1
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:40,873
Oh, lonesome night.

2
00:00:41,959 --> 00:00:46,921
And babbits bawling,
the wind biting the bone.

3
00:00:48,757 --> 00:00:50,591
Wind like this...

4
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:53,386
...full of voices.

5
00:00:55,138 --> 00:00:57,848
Ancestry howling at you...

6
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:00,851
...yibbering stories...

7
00:01:03,146 --> 00:01:07,817
...all voices tied up into one.

8
00:01:10,612 --> 00:01:12,613
One voice differing...

9
00:01:13,448 --> 00:01:15,032
...one voice...

10
00:01:16,451 --> 00:01:21,414
...whispering out there,
spying from the dark...

11
00:01:23,292 --> 00:01:26,669
...that fangy devil...

12
00:01:28,088 --> 00:01:30,590
...Old Georgie hisself.

13
00:01:31,758 --> 00:01:35,761
Mm. Now you ear up close...

14
00:01:36,638 --> 00:01:40,933
...and I'll yarn you about
the first time we met...

15
00:01:41,476 --> 00:01:43,728
...eye to eye.

16
00:01:47,983 --> 00:01:51,819
<i>EWING: And thus it was that I made
the acquaintance of Dr. Henry Goose...</i>

17
00:01:51,987 --> 00:01:54,947
<i>...the man I hoped might cure me
of my affliction.</i>

18
00:02:06,543 --> 00:02:07,960
Have you lost something?

19
00:02:10,422 --> 00:02:12,131
REY:
Question one:

20
00:02:12,591 --> 00:02:17,928
What secret in Sixsmith's report
would be worth killing him for?

21
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:20,139
Question two:

22
00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:25,144
Is it reasonable to believe that they
would kill again to protect that secret?

23
00:02:25,604 --> 00:02:30,066
And if so, question three:
What the fuck am I doing here?

24
00:02:33,070 --> 00:02:37,239
<i>CAVENDISH: While my extensive experience
as an editor has led me to a disdain...</i>

25
00:02:37,407 --> 00:02:41,619
<i>...for flashbacks and flash-forwards
and all such tricksy gimmicks...</i>

26
00:02:41,787 --> 00:02:45,831
<i>...I believe that if you, dear reader, can
extend your patience for just a moment...</i>

27
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:47,208
[TYPEWRITER DINGS]

28
00:02:47,376 --> 00:02:50,878
<i>...you will find there is a method
to this tale of madness.</i>

29
00:02:51,588 --> 00:02:53,506
<i>FROBISHER:
My Dearest Sixsmith:</i>

30
00:02:54,007 --> 00:02:57,093
<i>I shot myself through
the roof of my mouth this morning...</i>

31
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:59,053
<i>...with Vyvyan Ayrs' Luger.</i>

32
00:03:01,223 --> 00:03:06,519
<i>A true suicide is a paced,
disciplined certainty.</i>

33
00:03:07,813 --> 00:03:11,774
<i>People pontificate,
"Suicide is a coward's act. "</i>

34
00:03:12,651 --> 00:03:14,652
<i>Couldn't be further from the truth.</i>

35
00:03:15,696 --> 00:03:17,780
<i>Suicide takes tremendous courage.</i>

36
00:03:21,785 --> 00:03:25,037
MAN: Any problems, you just hit this button.
- Thank you.

37
00:03:29,793 --> 00:03:31,168
[DOOR CLOSES]

38
00:03:31,336 --> 00:03:35,381
On behalf of my ministry
and the future of Unanimity...

39
00:03:35,549 --> 00:03:38,884
...I would like to thank you
for this final interview.

40
00:03:39,761 --> 00:03:41,762
[ALL SHOUTING AND
CHANTING INDISTINCTLY]

41
00:03:48,145 --> 00:03:49,729
REY: Hello.
- Press pass.

42
00:03:51,022 --> 00:03:52,398
Expecting trouble?

43
00:03:52,566 --> 00:03:56,861
I was Dermot Hoggins' publisher,
not his shrink or his astrologer.

44
00:03:57,028 --> 00:03:59,488
And the ruddy, bloody truth is...

45
00:03:59,656 --> 00:04:02,742
...I had no idea what the man
was gonna do that night.

46
00:04:02,993 --> 00:04:06,996
This beach was once
a cannibal's banqueting hall...

47
00:04:07,748 --> 00:04:09,749
...where the strong gorged on the weak.

48
00:04:09,916 --> 00:04:12,501
But the teeth, sir, they spat out...

49
00:04:12,669 --> 00:04:16,380
...like you or I would expel
a cherry stone.

50
00:04:17,716 --> 00:04:21,552
Do you know the price
a quarter pound of these will earn?

51
00:04:22,679 --> 00:04:26,056
Remember, this is not
an interrogation or a trial.

52
00:04:27,517 --> 00:04:30,895
Your version of the truth
is all that matters.

53
00:04:32,397 --> 00:04:34,231
Truth is singular.

54
00:04:36,193 --> 00:04:39,570
Its versions are mistruths.

55
00:04:41,031 --> 00:04:43,574
<i>FROBISHER:
Don't let them say I killed myself for love.</i>

56
00:04:45,035 --> 00:04:47,620
<i>Had my infatuations,
but we both know in our hearts...</i>

57
00:04:47,788 --> 00:04:51,457
<i>...who is the sole love
of my short, bright life.</i>

58
00:05:18,652 --> 00:05:20,653
HORROX:
There you are, Mr. Ewing.

59
00:05:20,946 --> 00:05:23,405
As binding a covenant
there can ever be between men...

60
00:05:24,825 --> 00:05:26,826
...outside the province of scripture.

61
00:05:26,993 --> 00:05:28,577
Thank you, Reverend Horrox.

62
00:05:28,745 --> 00:05:31,789
I know my father-in-law is
profoundly excited about this deal.

63
00:05:31,957 --> 00:05:34,166
Haskell Moore is a great man.

64
00:05:34,668 --> 00:05:37,086
Future generations
depend on men like him...

65
00:05:37,254 --> 00:05:39,797
...men capable of speaking the truth.

66
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:41,841
Quite.

67
00:05:45,929 --> 00:05:48,264
When I first encountered
Haskell Moore's writing...

68
00:05:48,431 --> 00:05:52,518
...its perspicuity struck me as though
delivered through divine revelation.

69
00:05:52,686 --> 00:05:56,981
The learned doctor and I have spent many
a night debating Mr. Moore's tractus.

70
00:05:57,148 --> 00:05:59,859
I will concede that he makes a compelling
case as to why we are sitting here...

71
00:06:00,026 --> 00:06:01,861
...enjoying this divine lamb...

72
00:06:02,153 --> 00:06:04,238
...while Kupaka stands there...

73
00:06:04,406 --> 00:06:06,365
...content to serve.

74
00:06:06,658 --> 00:06:08,826
HORROX:
Indeed. Well, Kupaka...

75
00:06:09,119 --> 00:06:11,537
...you enjoy your life here with us...

76
00:06:11,705 --> 00:06:14,957
- ... do you not?
- Oh, yes, Reverend, sir.

77
00:06:15,125 --> 00:06:16,876
Kupaka very happy here.

78
00:06:17,127 --> 00:06:19,753
You see, this is Moore's
"Ladder of Civilization"...

79
00:06:19,921 --> 00:06:21,839
...the reason behind this natural order.

80
00:06:22,007 --> 00:06:23,424
Please, Giles, do shut up.

81
00:06:23,842 --> 00:06:25,801
I've been listening to this for weeks.

82
00:06:25,969 --> 00:06:29,305
I would love to know what his own
son-in-law has to say about it.

83
00:06:29,681 --> 00:06:31,140
Oh, well, let's see, uh...

84
00:06:32,183 --> 00:06:35,561
It is an inquiry concerning God's will
and the nature of men.

85
00:06:36,021 --> 00:06:38,647
And what does he have to say
about the nature of women?

86
00:06:38,815 --> 00:06:40,107
[HENRY & HORROX CHUCKLING]

87
00:06:40,275 --> 00:06:44,069
I'm afraid that's a subject
he prefers to pass by in silence.

88
00:06:44,571 --> 00:06:45,779
He wouldn't be the first.

89
00:06:46,531 --> 00:06:48,449
Uh, pray, Mr. Ewing, continue.

90
00:06:49,075 --> 00:06:52,494
Well, the question he does pose is...

91
00:06:52,662 --> 00:06:56,457
...if God created the world, how do we
know what things we can change...

92
00:06:56,666 --> 00:07:00,419
...and what things must remain
sacred and inviolable?

93
00:07:00,712 --> 00:07:01,879
[MEN GRUNTING]

94
00:07:02,047 --> 00:07:05,674
KUPAKA: Reverend Horrox
real specific how to run plantation.

95
00:07:05,842 --> 00:07:07,343
Georgian Way, best way, he say.

96
00:07:08,053 --> 00:07:10,512
Oh, God, this heat is unbearable.
How do they take it?

97
00:07:11,056 --> 00:07:15,684
Reverend Horrox say slaves
like camel, bred for desert.

98
00:07:15,936 --> 00:07:19,980
He say they not feel heat
like civilized folk.

99
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:22,566
Now we should get you out of the sun.

100
00:07:23,985 --> 00:07:25,194
And what...?

101
00:07:25,779 --> 00:07:27,696
What is that noise?

102
00:07:29,282 --> 00:07:31,283
[WHIP CRACKING]

103
00:08:32,470 --> 00:08:34,179
- Oh, there you are.
- What happened?

104
00:08:34,347 --> 00:08:36,306
Ah, ah, ah.

105
00:08:36,474 --> 00:08:39,643
It is as I suspected.
Gusano Coco Cervello...

106
00:08:40,103 --> 00:08:42,146
...better known as
the Polynesian Worm.

107
00:08:42,355 --> 00:08:45,149
I once saw a man's brain
after the worm finished with him.

108
00:08:45,316 --> 00:08:47,109
Maggoty cauliflower. Ugh.

109
00:08:47,277 --> 00:08:49,236
[CHUCKLES]

110
00:08:49,404 --> 00:08:50,446
But have no fear.

111
00:08:50,613 --> 00:08:53,866
This particular devil and I
are well-acquainted.

112
00:08:54,034 --> 00:08:55,159
Here we go.

113
00:08:55,577 --> 00:08:57,119
Ah, yes, yes.

114
00:08:57,287 --> 00:08:59,580
Oh, glug, glug, glug.

115
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,836
I don't know what I would have done
had our paths not crossed.

116
00:09:06,129 --> 00:09:07,796
Well, for starters, you would have died.

117
00:09:07,964 --> 00:09:09,631
[CHUCKLES]

118
00:09:09,924 --> 00:09:12,342
I shall find a way to repay you.

119
00:09:12,510 --> 00:09:15,137
Oh. No, unnecessary, I assure you.

120
00:09:15,346 --> 00:09:17,514
I am a doctor, Adam.

121
00:09:17,849 --> 00:09:20,350
A tiger cannot change its stripes.
Heh-heh-heh.

122
00:09:23,313 --> 00:09:27,941
<i>FROBISHER: Sixsmith, I do hope you will be
able to find it in your heart to forgive me.</i>

123
00:09:28,109 --> 00:09:30,110
[KNOCKING ON DOOR]

124
00:09:30,945 --> 00:09:32,362
MAN:
Mr. Frobisher!

125
00:09:34,449 --> 00:09:36,408
Mr. Robert Frobisher!

126
00:09:39,537 --> 00:09:41,747
Management would like
a word with you, please, sir!

127
00:09:44,584 --> 00:09:45,793
[KNOCKING CONTINUES]

128
00:09:46,836 --> 00:09:49,213
Mr. Frobisher, open this door, please!

129
00:09:50,673 --> 00:09:53,509
We do know you're in there, Mr. Frobisher.
Please comply!

130
00:09:57,972 --> 00:10:00,974
A letter is being drafted
to your father, sir.

131
00:10:11,778 --> 00:10:13,153
Mr. Frobisher!

132
00:10:14,572 --> 00:10:18,575
<i>FROBISHER: Hated leaving you like that.
Wasn't the goodbye I had in mind.</i>

133
00:10:19,077 --> 00:10:21,078
MAN:
Someone's in a hurry.

134
00:10:23,039 --> 00:10:26,583
<i>By the time you read this,
I will be on my way to Edinburgh.</i>

135
00:10:27,001 --> 00:10:29,503
<i>On my way to fame and fortune.</i>

136
00:10:29,921 --> 00:10:32,756
<i>I know you haven't heard of him,
but trust me:</i>

137
00:10:33,007 --> 00:10:36,885
<i>Vyvyan Ayrs is one of the musical greats,
Sixsmith.</i>

138
00:10:37,053 --> 00:10:41,140
<i>The tragedy is that he hasn't produced
any new work in years due to illness.</i>

139
00:10:41,391 --> 00:10:45,894
<i>My scheme is to persuade him
to hire me as his amanuensis...</i>

140
00:10:46,062 --> 00:10:48,522
<i>...and aide him in the creation
of a masterpiece...</i>

141
00:10:48,690 --> 00:10:50,065
<i>- Hey!
- ... before shooting up through...</i>

142
00:10:50,233 --> 00:10:51,483
<i>...the musical firmament...</i>

143
00:10:51,651 --> 00:10:56,155
<i>...eventually obliging Pater to admit that,
yes, the son he disinherited...</i>

144
00:10:56,322 --> 00:11:00,951
<i>...is none other than Robert Frobisher,
the greatest British composer of his time.</i>

145
00:11:01,911 --> 00:11:04,163
<i>I know, Sixsmith,
you groan and shake your head...</i>

146
00:11:04,330 --> 00:11:07,624
<i>...but you smile too,
which is why I love you.</i>

147
00:11:09,085 --> 00:11:10,335
<i>P.S.</i>

148
00:11:11,296 --> 00:11:13,046
<i>Thanks for the waistcoat.</i>

149
00:11:14,215 --> 00:11:16,717
<i>I needed something of yours
to keep me company.</i>

150
00:11:18,052 --> 00:11:20,220
AYRS:
St. George and the Dragon.

151
00:11:21,347 --> 00:11:24,474
Reminds me that
composing is a crusade.

152
00:11:24,642 --> 00:11:28,437
Sometimes you slay the dragon,
sometimes...

153
00:11:28,646 --> 00:11:30,647
...the dragon slays you.

154
00:11:32,525 --> 00:11:35,277
All right then. Frobisher, is it?

155
00:11:36,112 --> 00:11:38,989
I trust Mackeras taught you
enough to be useful.

156
00:11:39,949 --> 00:11:43,285
I've had this little melody for viola
rattling about my head for months.

157
00:11:43,453 --> 00:11:44,995
Let's see if you can get it down.

158
00:11:52,503 --> 00:11:54,504
[VOCALIZING]

159
00:11:58,468 --> 00:12:01,011
Subtle grace note before the third.

160
00:12:05,558 --> 00:12:07,392
Soft and simple, got it?

161
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:09,645
Now it gets interesting.

162
00:12:20,073 --> 00:12:22,616
Good. Play that back.

163
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:27,162
I'd love to, sir.

164
00:12:29,749 --> 00:12:31,166
Um...

165
00:12:31,334 --> 00:12:32,751
What key are we in?

166
00:12:32,919 --> 00:12:34,002
What key?

167
00:12:34,170 --> 00:12:35,629
G- minor, of course!

168
00:12:35,797 --> 00:12:37,881
And the time signature?

169
00:12:38,424 --> 00:12:41,134
For Christ's sake, did you hear it or not?

170
00:12:41,302 --> 00:12:44,179
- I just need a little more time-
- You need?

171
00:12:44,347 --> 00:12:46,765
My dear boy,
who is working for whom here?

172
00:12:46,933 --> 00:12:49,017
- I apologize, sir-
- Are you an amanuensis or an apologist?

173
00:12:49,185 --> 00:12:50,310
Now pay attention.

174
00:12:50,520 --> 00:12:54,189
3/4 changing to 4/4 on the fourth bar
and back to 3/4 on bar five...

175
00:12:54,357 --> 00:12:56,108
...if you can count that high.

176
00:12:56,276 --> 00:13:01,071
Crotchet G, pause for a quaver, repeat
G quaver, then E flat on the downbeat.

177
00:13:01,239 --> 00:13:03,240
[AYRS VOCALIZING]

178
00:13:03,408 --> 00:13:04,783
And so on.

179
00:13:05,326 --> 00:13:07,619
All right, let me hear it.

180
00:13:35,606 --> 00:13:37,607
Stop! Please, you're hurting me.

181
00:13:37,775 --> 00:13:40,944
You must have misheard me:
I said I had a melody, not a malady!

182
00:13:41,112 --> 00:13:42,195
JOCASTA:
Vyvyan?

183
00:13:42,363 --> 00:13:44,781
Jocasta! Deliver me.

184
00:13:45,325 --> 00:13:46,450
What's going on in here?

185
00:13:46,617 --> 00:13:48,785
AYRS:
An exercise in futility.

186
00:13:48,953 --> 00:13:50,120
Should I be introduced?

187
00:13:50,288 --> 00:13:53,290
There's really no point.
The boy's as useful as the clap.

188
00:13:53,750 --> 00:13:55,959
Fortunately, he'll be much easier
to get rid of.

189
00:13:56,127 --> 00:13:59,421
Would you be a dear
and get Henry to show the boy out?

190
00:13:59,589 --> 00:14:01,131
JOCASTA:
Yes, of course, darling.

191
00:14:01,716 --> 00:14:03,717
[PLAYING PIANO]

192
00:14:18,649 --> 00:14:19,649
It's beautiful.

193
00:14:22,362 --> 00:14:23,362
Yes.

194
00:14:24,614 --> 00:14:25,906
That's it!

195
00:14:26,491 --> 00:14:27,657
That's my melody!

196
00:14:31,245 --> 00:14:33,205
[ROCK MUSIC
PLAYING OVER SPEAKERS]

197
00:14:33,373 --> 00:14:36,958
Come on, Luisa!
We're meant to be together.

198
00:14:37,126 --> 00:14:39,336
Please, Luisa come on, come on.
I'm telling you.

199
00:14:39,504 --> 00:14:44,299
I'm telling you, baby, you can't leave me.
It's a past life thing or a future life thing.

200
00:14:44,467 --> 00:14:45,801
It's you and me.

201
00:14:45,968 --> 00:14:47,844
Look, for the last hour...

202
00:14:48,012 --> 00:14:50,806
...all I could think about was
throwing you off your balcony.

203
00:14:50,973 --> 00:14:52,724
Who the hell do you think you are?

204
00:14:52,892 --> 00:14:56,561
You write a bullshit column
for a fucking rag!

205
00:14:56,729 --> 00:14:57,896
Elevator!

206
00:15:01,484 --> 00:15:02,943
Thank you.

207
00:15:04,779 --> 00:15:06,488
[REY SIGHS]

208
00:15:06,656 --> 00:15:09,574
Nice to know the age of chivalry
isn't dead.

209
00:15:16,332 --> 00:15:17,332
[ELEVATOR RUMBLING]

210
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:19,501
[BOTH GRUNTING]

211
00:15:24,257 --> 00:15:25,549
You okay?

212
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:28,093
No bones broken, I think.

213
00:15:29,554 --> 00:15:31,680
No, no. You sit, you sit, you sit.

214
00:15:31,848 --> 00:15:33,306
Let me see.

215
00:15:41,858 --> 00:15:44,776
Great. Power outage.

216
00:15:45,486 --> 00:15:49,030
Perfect end to a perfect day.

217
00:15:50,199 --> 00:15:51,992
[SIGHS]

218
00:15:53,369 --> 00:15:55,704
Still glad the age of chivalry isn't dead?

219
00:15:57,832 --> 00:16:01,334
I'd still rather be right here
than back up there.

220
00:16:01,752 --> 00:16:03,920
Guess Mr. Capon
isn't everyone's cup of tea.

221
00:16:04,088 --> 00:16:08,550
Pfft. Guys like that are just
an occupational hazard.

222
00:16:10,511 --> 00:16:11,803
You were interviewing him?

223
00:16:11,971 --> 00:16:14,848
<i>Yeah, for Spyglass Magazine.</i>

224
00:16:16,058 --> 00:16:17,058
Luisa Rey.

225
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:19,478
Rufus Sixsmith.

226
00:16:20,313 --> 00:16:21,563
Rey?

227
00:16:24,484 --> 00:16:27,986
You wouldn't happen to be related
to the journalist Lester Rey?

228
00:16:28,654 --> 00:16:31,448
Yeah. He was my father.

229
00:16:31,616 --> 00:16:32,866
Really?

230
00:16:33,034 --> 00:16:36,953
He must have been enormously
proud of you, following in his footsteps.

231
00:16:37,371 --> 00:16:38,663
Mm.

232
00:16:40,041 --> 00:16:42,375
SIXSMITH:
That's her. My niece, Megan.

233
00:16:43,252 --> 00:16:44,586
She's lovely.

234
00:16:44,754 --> 00:16:49,424
A born physicist with a better mind
for mathematics than I ever had.

235
00:16:49,842 --> 00:16:53,094
Did her PhD at Cambridge.
A woman at Caius.

236
00:16:53,262 --> 00:16:55,931
Hm. Gives you hope for the world.

237
00:16:56,682 --> 00:16:58,683
[SIGHING]

238
00:16:58,851 --> 00:17:00,268
It's hot.

239
00:17:03,731 --> 00:17:06,066
And we're still here. Heh.

240
