00:32:41,668 --> 00:32:43,794
Zachry? Zachry!

475
00:32:43,962 --> 00:32:45,963
[HORSE NEIGHS]

476
00:32:54,222 --> 00:32:55,890
Stay here.

477
00:32:56,057 --> 00:32:57,725
Safe here.

478
00:32:58,518 --> 00:32:59,518
Zachry!

479
00:33:00,520 --> 00:33:01,645
[GRUNTS]

480
00:33:02,814 --> 00:33:07,067
Kona'll be feasting on Adam
and his boy by sunup.

481
00:33:07,235 --> 00:33:08,444
ADAM:
Zachry!

482
00:33:08,904 --> 00:33:10,237
No! Nay!

483
00:33:10,405 --> 00:33:12,031
Zachry!

484
00:33:12,199 --> 00:33:13,574
Zachry!

485
00:33:13,742 --> 00:33:15,784
You say all'a time, yeah?

486
00:33:16,077 --> 00:33:19,914
"The weak are meat,
the strong do eat"?

487
00:33:20,081 --> 00:33:22,750
Father! Aah! Unh.

488
00:33:22,918 --> 00:33:24,960
No! Jonas!

489
00:33:28,757 --> 00:33:31,050
OLD GEORGIE:
The True-true is what that is.

490
00:33:45,023 --> 00:33:49,944
<i>ZACHRY: Whole Valley whispering about
the blood of Adam and his son on my hands.</i>

491
00:33:50,111 --> 00:33:53,322
<i>But Rose and Catkin never believed
no yibbering, and stood by me.</i>

492
00:33:53,615 --> 00:33:54,949
Uncle Zach, look!

493
00:33:55,367 --> 00:33:57,535
Yeah, I see them.

494
00:33:58,828 --> 00:34:01,705
<i>Prescients come bartering twice a year...</i>

495
00:34:01,873 --> 00:34:04,250
<i>...their ships creep-crawling on waves...</i>

496
00:34:04,417 --> 00:34:07,461
<i>...just floating on
the Smart of the Old Uns.</i>

497
00:34:09,631 --> 00:34:10,965
Barter'll be starting soon.

498
00:34:11,216 --> 00:34:12,424
Yeah.

499
00:34:12,884 --> 00:34:14,760
You must go find your ma.

500
00:34:14,928 --> 00:34:16,929
[SHIP HORN BLARING]

501
00:34:17,931 --> 00:34:19,390
[SIGHS]

502
00:34:23,645 --> 00:34:25,062
What're you doing?

503
00:34:26,773 --> 00:34:29,108
Ma says ya ain't been right
since Sloosha's.

504
00:34:30,986 --> 00:34:33,404
Say I gotta keep eyewise on ya.

505
00:34:35,282 --> 00:34:37,408
You minding me while I mind the goats.

506
00:34:40,287 --> 00:34:41,495
I see.

507
00:34:42,872 --> 00:34:45,791
Stump is, who's gonna mind your ma
at the Barter?

508
00:34:46,251 --> 00:34:48,836
She got no tongue for haggling,
not like you and me.

509
00:34:49,004 --> 00:34:50,170
Sure you're all right?

510
00:34:50,380 --> 00:34:53,090
Swear'by. Be home for supping.

511
00:34:56,094 --> 00:35:00,180
<i>Why words slink'n slide off'a tongue
when we need them most?</i>

512
00:35:00,807 --> 00:35:03,601
<i>If my tongue been more bold...</i>

513
00:35:03,768 --> 00:35:06,979
<i>...could I'a stop all the diresomes
about to happen?</i>

514
00:35:09,024 --> 00:35:10,024
CATKIN:
Uncle Zach!

515
00:35:10,191 --> 00:35:12,026
This my big bro I yarning about.

516
00:35:12,819 --> 00:35:14,194
ZACHRY:
What's what, sis?

517
00:35:14,362 --> 00:35:15,988
Spesh guest hosting.

518
00:35:16,615 --> 00:35:19,408
Thank you for the kindsome host
of my Valley stay.

519
00:35:19,576 --> 00:35:21,493
I ain't yaysoed this, Rose.

520
00:35:21,870 --> 00:35:24,204
Abbess sayso a gift of great honor.

521
00:35:24,372 --> 00:35:25,831
She can be hosting then.

522
00:35:29,502 --> 00:35:31,211
I bring you gift, Zachry.

523
00:35:32,714 --> 00:35:34,423
Need no gift from a stranger.

524
00:35:37,302 --> 00:35:40,179
<i>ZACHRY: Now kin 'n bros 'n half-strangers,
even the Abbess...</i>

525
00:35:40,347 --> 00:35:42,264
<i>...all come knocking...</i>

526
00:35:42,432 --> 00:35:46,602
<i>...to gape in wonderment, like Sonmi herself
were sitting in our kitchen.</i>

527
00:35:47,145 --> 00:35:51,565
<i>Questions about Prescience and their
woahsome ship poured thick 'n fast.</i>

528
00:35:51,733 --> 00:35:54,401
How your ship slide 'n glide
so silentsome?

529
00:35:54,653 --> 00:35:56,695
Fusion engines.

530
00:35:57,656 --> 00:35:59,490
<i>No one queried what
"fusion engines" was...</i>

531
00:35:59,658 --> 00:36:03,160
<i>...because they didn't want to look stupid
front of the gathering.</i>

532
00:36:03,370 --> 00:36:04,912
Fusion engines.

533
00:36:05,664 --> 00:36:08,624
<i>True really was,
Meronym answer the questions...</i>

534
00:36:08,792 --> 00:36:11,585
<i>...but no answer ever quenched
your curio.</i>

535
00:36:11,753 --> 00:36:15,255
<i>All that answering done was teached
everyone to not trust her.</i>

536
00:36:15,423 --> 00:36:17,424
<i>Nay, not a flea.</i>

537
00:36:17,592 --> 00:36:20,010
She a sly one.

538
00:36:21,930 --> 00:36:25,015
Scheming and worming herself in.

539
00:36:25,809 --> 00:36:29,269
Watch her, watch her close.

540
00:36:30,605 --> 00:36:33,941
She's got secrets.

541
00:36:51,126 --> 00:36:53,127
[TOOL BUZZING]

542
00:37:08,309 --> 00:37:09,309
[GRUNTS]

543
00:37:13,648 --> 00:37:15,315
[GASPS THEN PANTING]

544
00:37:18,820 --> 00:37:20,821
[KNOCKING ON DOOR]

545
00:37:21,322 --> 00:37:23,365
- Zachry Bailey!
- Sorrysome for waking you, Abbess.

546
00:37:23,533 --> 00:37:26,160
I dream something
diresome's gonna happen.

547
00:37:26,828 --> 00:37:28,579
Come in, come in.

548
00:37:29,831 --> 00:37:33,292
ABBESS:
Oh, let Sonmi guide your heart.

549
00:37:34,836 --> 00:37:39,339
Oh, I can hear her voice a'prayin' for you.

550
00:37:39,674 --> 00:37:43,886
Oh, Old Georgie's a'hungerin'
for your soul.

551
00:37:44,053 --> 00:37:46,013
Oh. I know'd it.

552
00:37:46,890 --> 00:37:49,933
- Spit'n cuss on your dreams.
- I know'd it.

553
00:37:50,101 --> 00:37:51,685
[GASPS]

554
00:37:57,025 --> 00:37:59,318
"Bridge a'broken', hide below.

555
00:38:00,779 --> 00:38:03,113
Hands'a'bleedin', can't let go.

556
00:38:03,281 --> 00:38:06,492
Enemy's sleeping, don't slit that throat. "

557
00:38:06,659 --> 00:38:08,535
[GASPS THEN PANTING]

558
00:38:08,703 --> 00:38:10,329
An auguring.

559
00:38:13,666 --> 00:38:15,167
Oh, Zachry.

560
00:38:16,419 --> 00:38:17,961
Trust Sonmi.

561
00:38:19,798 --> 00:38:24,718
Keep Her warning with you.
Nail it to your memory.

562
00:38:26,721 --> 00:38:29,306
Thank you. Thank you, Abbess.

563
00:38:29,724 --> 00:38:30,724
Thank you.

564
00:38:30,934 --> 00:38:32,851
<i>COMPUTERIZED VOICE:
Sonmi-451.</i>

565
00:38:33,019 --> 00:38:35,020
[COMPUTERIZED VOICE
SPEAKS IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

566
00:38:47,909 --> 00:38:49,910
[TOOL WHIRRING AND CLICKING]

567
00:38:53,248 --> 00:38:55,249
[TOOL BLEEPS]

568
00:38:58,002 --> 00:38:59,378
CHANG:
Wait, wait.

569
00:39:00,880 --> 00:39:03,173
There's no reason to hide.

570
00:39:06,094 --> 00:39:10,472
I know you are Sonmi-451.

571
00:39:12,851 --> 00:39:15,602
My name is Hae-Joo Chang.

572
00:39:16,855 --> 00:39:19,523
What has happened to Seer Rhee?

573
00:39:20,483 --> 00:39:21,775
CHANG:
Soap overdose.

574
00:39:22,610 --> 00:39:26,738
It is unfortunate that it had to happen
with everything going so well...

575
00:39:27,699 --> 00:39:31,118
...because now it is probable that
the Enforcers and the DNA sniffers...

576
00:39:31,286 --> 00:39:32,995
...will find out about you.

577
00:39:33,371 --> 00:39:37,499
And if they do, if they realize
your connection to Yoona-939...

578
00:39:37,667 --> 00:39:39,418
...you will be excised.

579
00:39:40,753 --> 00:39:42,379
But you have a choice:

580
00:39:43,798 --> 00:39:47,634
You can remain here
and risk being discovered...

581
00:39:50,638 --> 00:39:52,347
...or you can come with me.

582
00:39:53,308 --> 00:39:54,892
MOLYNEUX:
The foresail!

583
00:39:55,727 --> 00:39:57,227
OLD SALTY DOG:
Two, six, heave!

584
00:39:57,395 --> 00:39:58,687
Two, six, heave!

585
00:39:58,855 --> 00:40:00,063
MOLYNEUX:
Close the gaskets!

586
00:40:02,442 --> 00:40:04,026
OLD SALTY DOG:
Cape home!

587
00:40:05,194 --> 00:40:06,236
Bear away a point.

588
00:40:06,404 --> 00:40:07,404
Aye, aye, captain!

589
00:40:07,572 --> 00:40:08,655
MAN:
And hurry!

590
00:40:10,867 --> 00:40:14,786
<i>EWING: Friday the 15th.
We made sail with the morning tide.</i>

591
00:40:15,663 --> 00:40:18,165
<i>Mr. Boerhaave had my cabin changed.</i>

592
00:40:18,333 --> 00:40:22,252
<i>I have been quarantined to a storeroom
away from the other passengers and crew.</i>

593
00:40:22,754 --> 00:40:26,673
<i>Henry argued in vain that the
Polynesian Worm is not contagious.</i>

594
00:40:26,841 --> 00:40:28,175
<i>Hardly matters.</i>

595
00:40:28,343 --> 00:40:32,471
<i>All I want to do now is return home
and unburden myself of this responsibility.</i>

596
00:40:35,183 --> 00:40:38,602
<i>FROBISHER: My Dear Sixsmith,
I am in desperate need of your help.</i>

597
00:40:39,020 --> 00:40:42,105
<i>After my last letter,
I'm sure you're rushing to pack your bags...</i>

598
00:40:42,273 --> 00:40:43,607
<i>...but you needn't, really...</i>

599
00:40:43,775 --> 00:40:48,570
<i>...unless, of course, you wish to witness
the rebirth of Robert Frobisher.</i>

600
00:40:50,448 --> 00:40:52,824
<i>Is it not miraculous how one's fortune...</i>

601
00:40:52,992 --> 00:40:56,119
<i>...can turn so quickly, so completely?</i>

602
00:40:57,705 --> 00:41:00,207
<i>One moment,
leaping from a hotel window...</i>

603
00:41:00,375 --> 00:41:05,087
<i>...the next, gainfully employed by one
of the world's greatest living composers.</i>

604
00:41:05,838 --> 00:41:10,133
<i>My only problem is that I accidentally
got hooked on a journal written in 1849...</i>

605
00:41:10,301 --> 00:41:14,930
<i>...by a dying lawyer during the voyage
from a Pacific Isle to San Francisco.</i>

606
00:41:15,306 --> 00:41:19,810
<i>To my great annoyance, the pages cease
mid-sentence. Half the book is missing.</i>

607
00:41:19,978 --> 00:41:21,645
<i>It's completely killing me.</i>

608
00:41:21,813 --> 00:41:22,854
<i>Could you be a mensch...</i>

609
00:41:23,022 --> 00:41:26,233
<i>...and when you're next foraging
at Otto's Books, make an inquiry?</i>

610
00:41:26,526 --> 00:41:31,446
<i>A half-finished book is, after all,
a half-finished love affair.</i>

611
00:41:31,823 --> 00:41:35,409
<i>MAN [ON TV]: -Schuhmann and
Tom Marshall, my chief engineers...</i>

612
00:41:35,576 --> 00:41:38,078
<i>...and then we'll take some questions.</i>

613
00:41:38,246 --> 00:41:41,498
<i>America loves oil.</i>

614
00:41:41,749 --> 00:41:44,835
<i>America is addicted to oil.</i>

615
00:41:45,253 --> 00:41:49,506
<i>Some fantasize about
wind turbines or pig gas.</i>

616
00:41:49,674 --> 00:41:50,841
[CROWD LAUGHS ON TV]

617
00:41:51,009 --> 00:41:56,179
<i>But I'm here today to tell you
that the cure for oil is right here.</i>

618
00:41:56,347 --> 00:42:00,684
<i>The cure is nuclear power.
The cure is Swannekke.</i>

619
00:42:00,852 --> 00:42:02,019
[CROWD APPLAUDING ON TV]

620
00:42:02,186 --> 00:42:04,187
[PHONE RINGING]

621
00:42:08,860 --> 00:42:10,819
- Hello?
SIXSMITH: Hello, Ms. Rey.

622
00:42:10,987 --> 00:42:13,947
<i>I'm frightfully sorry
for calling at this hour.</i>

623
00:42:14,115 --> 00:42:15,782
Dr. Sixsmith?

624
00:42:15,950 --> 00:42:17,409
<i>I need help.</i>

625
00:42:17,785 --> 00:42:19,953
I need 50,000 pounds!

626
00:42:20,121 --> 00:42:22,581
Not two thousand, 50 thousand!

627
00:42:22,790 --> 00:42:25,375
<i>WOMAN: I can go through it again,
Mr. Cavendish, but the total's right.</i>

628
00:42:25,543 --> 00:42:27,794
<i>2,343 pounds and 16 pence.</i>

629
00:42:27,962 --> 00:42:31,798
How is this possible?!
The ruddy money was pouring in.

630
00:42:31,966 --> 00:42:34,843
<i>Debts mostly, Mr. Cavendish.
Solvency has its drawbacks.</i>

631
00:42:35,011 --> 00:42:36,428
[SIGHS THEN GRUNTS]

632
00:42:37,722 --> 00:42:39,848
<i>CAVENDISH:
The situation looked dire...</i>

633
00:42:40,308 --> 00:42:44,436
<i>...but I knew that Timothy Cavendish's
capital in this town...</i>

634
00:42:44,604 --> 00:42:47,314
<i>...could not be defined by
an accountant's balance sheet.</i>

635
00:42:47,482 --> 00:42:51,151
McCluskie! Look, how are those
delightful kiddies of yours?

636
00:42:51,319 --> 00:42:53,153
[LINE DISCONNECTS]

637
00:42:53,321 --> 00:42:54,321
[CAVENDISH SPEAKS IN FRENCH]

638
00:42:54,489 --> 00:42:58,742
It's "Cavendish the Ravenous,"
ha-ha-ha, your favorite Timothy.

639
00:42:58,910 --> 00:43:00,368
[LINE DISCONNECTS]

640
00:43:00,536 --> 00:43:01,536
[SPEAKS IN FRENCH]

641
00:43:01,704 --> 00:43:02,704
You heard correctly.

642
00:43:02,872 --> 00:43:07,125
Charles Dickens' own, original,
authentic writing desk for 60,000 pounds.

643
00:43:07,335 --> 00:43:08,376
I think that's very fair.

644
00:43:08,544 --> 00:43:10,837
<i>WOMAN: But our records indicate
that the desk is already accounted for...</i>

645
00:43:11,005 --> 00:43:12,005
<i>...by the Dickens House Museum.</i>

646
00:43:12,340 --> 00:43:14,841
Okay, what about
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's desk?

647
00:43:15,009 --> 00:43:16,301
[LINE DISCONNECTS]

648
00:43:16,677 --> 00:43:18,678
[SIGHS]

649
00:43:23,142 --> 00:43:26,520
<i>CAVENDISH:
In the darkness, I suddenly saw the light.</i>

650
00:43:27,438 --> 00:43:30,065
<i>Blood has always trumped water.</i>

651
00:43:30,233 --> 00:43:32,984
<i>If the Hogginses brutes wanted
to turn this into a family affair...</i>

652
00:43:33,152 --> 00:43:38,073
<i>...they'd find the Cavendish clan
more than ready for the task at hand.</i>

653
00:43:40,743 --> 00:43:45,372
Oh, Satan's gonads, not again.
Look, just bugger off and leave us in peace.

654
00:43:45,540 --> 00:43:48,125
- I'm only gonna ask you nicely once.
- Good to see you, Denny.

655
00:43:48,292 --> 00:43:52,003
I'm not lending you a ruddy farthing
until you pay back the last lot.

656
00:43:52,171 --> 00:43:54,923
Why should I be forever
giving you handouts?

657
00:43:55,091 --> 00:43:59,386
Denny, I've had a minor run-in
with the wrong sort.

658
00:43:59,554 --> 00:44:04,141
If I don't get my hands on 60,000 pounds,
I'm going to take an awful beating.

659
00:44:04,308 --> 00:44:07,018
Well, get them to video it for us,
would you? Now fuck off.

660
00:44:07,270 --> 00:44:09,020
I'm not joking, Denholme.

661
00:44:09,188 --> 00:44:10,647
Why is this my problem?

662
00:44:10,815 --> 00:44:13,233
Because we're brothers!
Don't you have a conscience?

663
00:44:14,569 --> 00:44:18,155
Couple of my special little pills
and a G & T should set me right.

664
00:44:18,322 --> 00:44:19,406
Denny...

665
00:44:19,740 --> 00:44:20,782
...help.

666
00:44:23,452 --> 00:44:24,703
Please?

667
00:44:26,247 --> 00:44:28,248
Den, who are you talking to?

668
00:44:28,416 --> 00:44:29,916
Hello, Georgette.

669
00:44:30,751 --> 00:44:31,751
Hello, Timothy.

670
00:44:37,300 --> 00:44:40,552
Right. All right. What, 60 grand?

671
00:44:40,720 --> 00:44:43,471
It's gonna take some time,
but in the interim...

672
00:44:43,639 --> 00:44:46,308
...I've got the perfect place
for you to hide.

673
00:44:46,809 --> 00:44:48,101
MAN 1:
Rise tacks and sheets!

674
00:44:48,269 --> 00:44:52,939
<i>EWING: I have begun to fear I may never
hold my beloved Tilda in my arms again.</i>

675
00:44:53,566 --> 00:44:57,611
<i>The Parasite writhes at night,
igniting spasms of pain...</i>

676
00:44:58,070 --> 00:45:00,989
<i>...while visions and voices,
obscene and monstrous, haunt me.</i>

677
00:45:01,157 --> 00:45:02,741
MAN 2:
Mr. Ewing?

678
00:45:03,492 --> 00:45:06,453
In the name of God! Help, help!

679
00:45:06,746 --> 00:45:10,540
Mr. Ewing, no fear, no harm, no shout...

680
00:45:10,875 --> 00:45:11,875
...please.

681
00:45:12,793 --> 00:45:14,211
My name Autua.

682
00:45:14,378 --> 00:45:17,881
You know I, you seen Maori whip I.

683
00:45:19,133 --> 00:45:20,467
You know I.

684
00:45:23,054 --> 00:45:24,429
What do you want?

685
00:45:24,597 --> 00:45:26,348
You help, Mr. Ewing.

686
00:45:26,974 --> 00:45:29,476
If you no help, I in trouble dead.

687
00:45:30,269 --> 00:45:32,729
Well, you're already very much
in trouble dead.

688
00:45:32,897 --> 00:45:36,983
The Prophetess is a mercantile vessel, not
an underground railroad for escaping slaves.

689
00:45:37,193 --> 00:45:40,237
I able seaman. I earn passage.

690
00:45:40,404 --> 00:45:42,906
Well, then I suggest you surrender
to the captain's mercies forthwith.

691
00:45:43,074 --> 00:45:44,699
No, no!

692
00:45:45,159 --> 00:45:46,409
They no hear I!

693
00:45:47,703 --> 00:45:50,747
They say swim away home, nigger,
and throw I in drink!

694
00:45:50,915 --> 00:45:52,582
But you lawman, aye?

695
00:45:52,750 --> 00:45:55,085
Please, cap'n hear you, Mr. Ewing.

696
00:45:55,253 --> 00:45:56,795
No, I can't help you.

697
00:45:57,421 --> 00:46:01,007
I'm afraid your fate is entirely your own.
I desire no part in it.

698
00:46:03,511 --> 00:46:05,011
Then kill I.

699
00:46:05,429 --> 00:46:07,097
Don't be absurd.

700
00:46:07,515 --> 00:46:12,102
If you no help, you kill I just the same.
It's true, you know it.

701
00:46:12,353 --> 00:46:14,813
I ain't be no fish food, Mr. Ewing.

702
00:46:15,022 --> 00:46:17,732
Die here better. Do it.

703
00:46:19,694 --> 00:46:21,069
Do it quick.

704
00:46:22,321 --> 00:46:24,614
WOMAN:
I found an old Transway marker, captain.

705
00:46:25,866 --> 00:46:27,534
It's got to be the right mountain.

706
00:46:28,828 --> 00:46:30,954
Problem is, the Valley people
are afraid of it.

707
00:46:31,122 --> 00:46:32,747
They think the devil lives up there.

708
00:46:33,332 --> 00:46:36,918
I can't find anybody to guide me
through the Kona territory.

709
00:46:37,086 --> 00:46:38,128
<i>Meronym...</i>

710
00:46:38,296 --> 00:46:39,296
[MAN SPEAKS
IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

711
00:46:39,463 --> 00:46:42,507
<i>...every day you are out there,
you increase your RAD levels.</i>

712
00:46:42,675 --> 00:46:45,093
<i>This dream of yours is going to kill you.</i>

713
00:46:45,261 --> 00:46:49,681
<i>And for what? The off-world colonies
may no longer exist.</i>

714
00:46:50,057 --> 00:46:51,057
I gotta go, captain.

715
00:46:54,145 --> 00:46:56,396
Thank you for coming. I'm in 1404.

716
00:46:56,564 --> 00:46:58,064
<i>REY:
I'll be right up.</i>

717
00:47:24,383 --> 00:47:26,384
[SILENCED GUNSHOT]

718
00:47:34,852 --> 00:47:36,394
[KNOCKING ON DOOR]

719
00:47:37,646 --> 00:47:39,147
REY:
Dr. Sixsmith?

720
00:47:48,491 --> 00:47:49,824
Dr. Sixsmith?

721
00:47:52,119 --> 00:47:53,495
It's Luisa.

722
00:48:25,820 --> 00:48:27,821
[DOOR BEEPS]

723
00:48:35,538 --> 00:48:37,539
[BOTH GASP]

724
00:48:40,793 --> 00:48:43,461
Go call the police, right now.
Call the police!

725
00:48:46,799 --> 00:48:48,091
Oh, God.

726
00:49:19,832 --> 00:49:24,127
<i>FROBISHER: My Dear Sixsmith, you alone
could understand how I'm feeling right now.</i>

727
00:49:24,712 --> 00:49:29,591
<i>Today, Ayrs and I presented our first
collaboration to Tadeusz Kesselring...</i>

728
00:49:29,758 --> 00:49:32,760
<i>...Ayrs' favorite conductor,
who arrived from Berlin.</i>

729
00:49:33,721 --> 00:49:36,764
<i>It's called "Eternal Recurrence. "
Wish you could hear it.</i>

730
00:49:36,932 --> 00:49:40,226
<i>It's the most accomplished tone poem
I know of written since the war...</i>

731
00:49:40,394 --> 00:49:44,355
<i>...and I tell you, Sixsmith, that more than
a few of its best ideas are mine.</i>

732
00:49:48,819 --> 00:49:55,241
At our time of life, Ayrs, a man
has no right to such daring ideas.

733
00:49:55,409 --> 00:49:56,784
[KESSELRING & AYRS CHUCKLE]

734
00:49:57,536 --> 00:50:03,333
I suppose I've won a rearguard action
or two in my war against decrepitude.

735
00:50:03,626 --> 00:50:06,878
<i>FROBISHER: Dinner ofpheasant
and Bordeaux, rich as butter-cream.</i>

736
00:50:07,671 --> 00:50:10,215
<i>How I love to listen to men
of distinguished lives...</i>

737
00:50:10,382 --> 00:50:13,176
<i>...sing of past follies and glories.</i>

738
00:50:13,344 --> 00:50:16,971
<i>The only broken note in the entire
evening was Ayrs' wife, Jocasta...</i>

739
00:50:17,348 --> 00:50:19,349
<i>...excusing herself early.</i>

740
00:50:20,809 --> 00:50:22,101
<i>Sensed a buried bone.</i>

741
00:50:22,811 --> 00:50:24,521
<i>Later I asked Ayrs about it.</i>

742
00:50:24,688 --> 00:50:27,690
<i>He said Kesselring had
introduced Jocasta to him.</i>

743
00:50:27,983 --> 00:50:31,486
<i>I pried, "Had Kesselring
been in love with her?"</i>

744
00:50:31,946 --> 00:50:33,905
<i>The subject was a prickly one.</i>

745
00:50:34,073 --> 00:50:35,782
Jocasta is a Jew.

746
00:50:36,283 --> 00:50:38,618
So obviously,
a relationship was impossible.

747
00:50:39,370 --> 00:50:40,912
Why "obviously"?

748
00:50:42,164 --> 00:50:46,417
Can you really be so ignorant
of what is happening in Germany?

749
00:50:47,670 --> 00:50:50,880
<i>At this point in my life,
all I know, Sixsmith...</i>

750
00:50:51,298 --> 00:50:55,510
<i>...is that this world spins
from the same unseen forces...</i>

751
00:50:55,928 --> 00:50:58,012
<i>...that twist our hearts.</i>

752
00:50:59,056 --> 00:51:00,515
How is it'n, Zachry?

753
00:51:00,891 --> 00:51:02,308
Samewise.

754
00:51:02,810 --> 00:51:04,269
MERONYM:
Mindin' some comp'ny?

755
00:51:04,436 --> 00:51:05,645
ZACHRY:
Nay.

756
00:51:05,896 --> 00:51:10,817
But goats'n surlywise herders
ain't known for our howziting temper.

757
00:51:13,362 --> 00:51:18,324
Feelin' I ownin' you a real kowtow
for 'vadin' y'house with no sayso.

758
00:51:18,826 --> 00:51:20,326
True sorrysome.

759
00:51:21,579 --> 00:51:23,705
Fuggit, done's done.

760
00:51:24,331 --> 00:51:28,126
MERONYM: So, you mindin' a stranger
queryin' 'bout your troddin'?

761
00:51:29,420 --> 00:51:32,255
Swap you, query for query.

762
00:51:34,717 --> 00:51:35,883
Fair'by.

763
00:51:36,343 --> 00:51:40,430
Cog y'ain't come t'learn stitchin'
or milking or herding.

764
00:51:42,349 --> 00:51:43,516
Why you here?

765
00:51:44,852 --> 00:51:46,561
I needing a guide.

766
00:51:47,646 --> 00:51:49,606
Guide? To what?

767
00:51:51,025 --> 00:51:52,650
Mauna Sol.

768
00:52:00,534 --> 00:52:03,328
<i>ABBESS:
"Bridge a'broken, hide below. "</i>

769
00:52:05,623 --> 00:52:06,623
What's wrong?

770
00:52:27,728 --> 00:52:29,228
[HORSE NEIGHS]

771
00:52:36,737 --> 00:52:40,740
<i>CAVENDISH: We cross and recross
our old tracks like figure skaters...</i>

772
00:52:40,908 --> 00:52:43,534
<i>...and just as I was reading
a new submission...</i>

773
00:52:43,994 --> 00:52:47,872
<i>...a powerful déjà-vu
ran through my bones.</i>

774
00:52:48,916 --> 00:52:54,671
<i>I had been here before,
another lifetime ago.</i>

775
00:52:55,839 --> 00:52:57,215
<i>Ursula...</i>

776
00:52:58,717 --> 00:53:00,593
<i>...the love of my life?</i>

777
00:53:01,345 --> 00:53:04,138
<i>I could think of no other
serious applicants.</i>

778
00:53:04,973 --> 00:53:06,974
<i>What had happened to her?</i>

779
00:53:07,309 --> 00:53:11,604
<i>And more importantly,
what had happened to the young man...</i>

780
00:53:11,772 --> 00:53:14,982
<i>...who had ridden this same train
composing sonnets...</i>

781
00:53:15,526 --> 00:53:18,403
<i>...to his "Soul Bound Love"?</i>

782
00:53:18,570 --> 00:53:22,740
The auguring come true, Abbess.
Broke bridge just like you say.

783
00:53:22,950 --> 00:53:25,785
Meronym were there yibbering
about trekking up Mauna Sol...

784
00:53:25,953 --> 00:53:29,163
...horses'pess'n, woman come
cussing n'twisting up my life?

785
00:53:29,331 --> 00:53:30,998
Mind the Words o'Sonmi.

786
00:53:34,253 --> 00:53:37,296
"Our lives are not our own.

787
00:53:38,590 --> 00:53:42,343
<i>From womb to tomb,
we are bound to others...</i>

788
00:53:44,054 --> 00:53:45,722
<i>...past and present...</i>

789
00:53:49,143 --> 00:53:51,185
<i>...and by each crime...</i>

790
00:53:53,272 --> 00:53:54,981
<i>...and every kindness...</i>

791
00:53:56,734 --> 00:53:58,526
<i>...we birth our future. "</i>

792
00:54:06,994 --> 00:54:09,829
Welcome to Neo Seoul.

793
00:54:13,667 --> 00:54:15,668
[MAN SHOUTING INDISTINCTLY]

794
00:54:22,509 --> 00:54:23,509
Come on, out you come.

795
00:54:26,138 --> 00:54:27,889
This may be the biggest mistake
of my life, but here.

796
00:54:28,056 --> 00:54:30,892
Thank you, Mr. Ewing. Thank you.

797
00:54:33,395 --> 00:54:35,146
Now to tell the truth, I was worried...

798
00:54:35,314 --> 00:54:37,857
...you might try and eat me if you didn't
get something in that stomach.

799
00:54:38,400 --> 00:54:41,903
Well, you safe, Mr. Ewing.
I no like white meat.

800
00:54:43,947 --> 00:54:46,240
- Ha-ha-ha.
- Oh. Right.

801
00:54:47,451 --> 00:54:50,828
But before I decide what
I am going to do with you...

802
00:54:52,790 --> 00:54:55,374
...tell me why you were
being whipped so savagely.

803
00:54:55,709 --> 00:54:57,585
My uncle was a sailor.

804
00:54:58,045 --> 00:55:00,963
He took me on a French whaler
when I was 10 years old.

805
00:55:01,298 --> 00:55:03,549
I seen too much of the world.

806
00:55:04,384 --> 00:55:06,177
I no good slave.

807
00:55:07,930 --> 00:55:09,555
Why did you look at me?

808
00:55:11,683 --> 00:55:15,603
Pain strong, aye?
But friend's eye more strong.

809
00:55:17,064 --> 00:55:20,733
Look, you are a runaway slave
and I am a lawyer.

810
00:55:21,652 --> 00:55:24,153
How do you imagine
we could possibly be friends?

811
00:55:29,284 --> 00:55:30,618
All you need.

812
00:55:36,583 --> 00:55:38,584
[DOOR OPENS]

813
00:55:41,505 --> 00:55:45,091
Jesus! Javier Gomez, what did I tell you
about jumping onto my balcony?

814
00:55:45,259 --> 00:55:47,677
Why do you leave the door open
if you don't want me to come in?

815
00:55:47,845 --> 00:55:51,389
Because, smarty-pants, the only thing worse
than having you jump onto my balcony...

816
00:55:51,723 --> 00:55:55,101
...is the idea of you jumping onto
my balcony and being stuck out there.

817
00:55:55,269 --> 00:55:56,769
Okay.

818
00:55:57,813 --> 00:55:58,813
What are you reading?

819
00:56:01,441 --> 00:56:03,651
Just old letters.

820
00:56:04,528 --> 00:56:05,820
<i>FROBISHER:
Sixsmith...</i>

821
00:56:06,405 --> 00:56:09,156
<i>The plot has taken a sensual turn.</i>

822
00:56:10,367 --> 00:56:12,743
<i>Last week,
Jocasta and I became lovers.</i>

823
00:56:13,370 --> 00:56:17,373
<i>But don't alarm yourself. It is only
a carnal act performed in service.</i>

824
00:56:17,624 --> 00:56:20,251
<i>Not unlike my role as amanuensis.</i>

825
00:56:20,961 --> 00:56:25,965
<i>And I confess, women's hearts,
like their desire, remain a mystery to me.</i>

826
00:56:29,803 --> 00:56:34,056
<i>Afterward she cried and thanked me
for bringing life back into their home...</i>

827
00:56:34,224 --> 00:56:37,768
<i>...making it clear that Vyvyan
had been there the entire night...</i>

828
00:56:37,936 --> 00:56:42,857
<i>...between us like the silence between
notes that holds the key to all music.</i>

829
00:56:46,528 --> 00:56:49,030
<i>P.S. Best news of all:</i>

830
00:56:49,531 --> 00:56:51,532
<i>I've started my own work.</i>

831
00:56:55,162 --> 00:56:56,829
[CLASSICAL MUSIC PLAYING]

832
00:56:56,997 --> 00:57:01,459
Uh, I called about an old recording...

833
00:57:01,668 --> 00:57:04,962
...written by a man named
Robert Frobisher.

834
00:57:05,255 --> 00:57:07,840
Oops. Uh, busted.

835
00:57:08,258 --> 00:57:09,508
I know I shouldn't be playing it.

836
00:57:09,676 --> 00:57:13,721
I was checking it to make sure
it wasn't scratched.

837
00:57:13,889 --> 00:57:17,683
But, honestly,
I just can't stop listening to it.

838
00:57:18,226 --> 00:57:20,227
This is the "Cloud Atlas Sextet"?

839
00:57:22,272 --> 00:57:24,523
It's the "Symphony. "

840
00:57:29,821 --> 00:57:31,238
It's beautiful.

841
00:57:31,907 --> 00:57:34,241
But I think I heard this before.

842
00:57:34,409 --> 00:57:35,868
I can't imagine how.

843
00:57:36,370 --> 00:57:40,414
I doubt there's more than a handful
of copies in all of North America.

844
00:57:40,874 --> 00:57:42,416
But I know it.

845
00:57:44,711 --> 00:57:46,379
I know I know it.

846
00:57:46,797 --> 00:57:50,508
<i>CAVENDISH: Before I realized it,
my feet had borne me back...</i>

847
00:57:50,717 --> 00:57:55,179
<i>...to the Temple of Sacrifice
where I offered up my virginity.</i>

848
00:57:55,347 --> 00:57:57,598
<i>Back to those four days of paradise...</i>

849
00:57:57,766 --> 00:58:04,105
<i>...when Ursula's mater and pater slipped off
to Greece for a long weekend.</i>

850
00:58:07,442 --> 00:58:09,360
<i>Or so we thought.</i>

851
00:58:09,736 --> 00:58:11,237
[ALL GASP]

852
00:58:11,405 --> 00:58:12,738
- Ursula!
- Oh!

853
00:58:15,325 --> 00:58:16,325
[CAT CATERWAULING]

854
00:58:16,493 --> 00:58:19,620
Sir, madam! I assure you
this is completely innocent!

855
00:58:19,788 --> 00:58:21,914
[BOTH SCREAMING]

856
00:58:22,082 --> 00:58:24,250
[BOTH SHRIEK]

857
00:58:24,418 --> 00:58:27,211
<i>Two sprained ankles, one cracked rib.</i>

858
00:58:27,379 --> 00:58:31,966
<i>Official cause of accident listed
on the hospital form, "pussy. "</i>

859
00:58:35,887 --> 00:58:39,390
<i>What were the chances that
she still lived in this house?</i>

860
00:58:41,268 --> 00:58:43,102
<i>And yet, there she was.</i>

861
00:58:44,479 --> 00:58:45,855
<i>Ursula.</i>

862
00:58:46,940 --> 00:58:50,192
<i>Why had I never returned
her calls or letters?</i>

863
00:58:50,527 --> 00:58:55,281
<i>Shame. Spinelessness.
Hallmarks of the Cavendish clan.</i>

864
00:58:56,825 --> 00:58:58,451
<i>I realized I had a choice.</i>

865
00:58:59,036 --> 00:59:01,912
<i>I could slink off
and continue as planned...</i>

866
00:59:02,080 --> 00:59:06,876
<i>...or I could go boldly to the door
and discover what hope, if any, lay within.</i>

867
00:59:09,171 --> 00:59:12,048
- Abbess, come quick. It's the Bailey girl.
- Catkin?

868
00:59:12,215 --> 00:59:13,799
Aye. She dying.

869
00:59:17,721 --> 00:59:19,638
- What happened?
ROSE: Zach!

870
00:59:23,143 --> 00:59:25,811
- Sonmi...
- The scorpion fish.

871
00:59:25,979 --> 00:59:28,856
Healer say she be gone by sundown.

872
00:59:29,316 --> 00:59:32,234
Ain't right, ain't fair
but nothing to be done.

873
00:59:41,328 --> 00:59:42,953
OVID:
Be very still.

874
00:59:43,330 --> 00:59:44,997
Termination charge, here.

875
00:59:45,165 --> 00:59:46,499
[BEEPING]

876
00:59:46,666 --> 00:59:49,585
Just enough to blow carotid.

877
00:59:49,753 --> 00:59:51,504
[TOOL WHIRRING]

878
01:00:16,988 --> 01:00:18,572
How does it feel?

879
01:00:20,951 --> 01:00:22,076
Good.

880
01:00:25,997 --> 01:00:27,540
Catkin's dying!

881
01:00:28,250 --> 01:00:30,543
- What?
- Trod on a scorpion fish.

882
01:00:32,379 --> 01:00:35,923
You can save her. You got spesh smarts
in that gearbag what'll save her.

883
01:00:36,091 --> 01:00:37,675
That's the True-true.

884
01:00:37,884 --> 01:00:40,678
Prescient Council swear
by special order...

885
01:00:40,887 --> 01:00:44,140
...say I can't go play Lady Sonmi
for every fate twistin' wrong...

886
01:00:44,307 --> 01:00:45,766
...n' click fingers make right.

887
01:00:46,935 --> 01:00:50,104
I just a stupid goat herder, but I cog
you killin' Catkin by not acting...

888
01:00:50,272 --> 01:00:53,566
...just as if I left you up on that bridge,
you Kona meat.

889
01:00:55,902 --> 01:01:00,114
If a Prescient be laying with poison
melting her heart'n'lungs...

890
01:01:00,407 --> 01:01:02,116
...if it be your kin?

891
01:01:03,660 --> 01:01:06,537
Why's a Prescient's life
worth more'n a Valleysman?

892
01:01:16,173 --> 01:01:17,548
I'll take you to Mauna Sol.

893
01:01:18,592 --> 01:01:20,134
I know the way.

894
01:01:20,719 --> 01:01:22,678
If you save Catkin...

895
01:01:23,638 --> 01:01:27,141
...I will guide you through the devil's door
if that's where you want to go.

896
01:01:32,147 --> 01:01:35,691
<i>CAVENDISH: While the past may call to us
with the enchantment of a siren...</i>

897
01:01:37,110 --> 01:01:41,655
<i>...better, I believed, to stay the course
set by dear brother Denny.</i>

898
01:01:41,823 --> 01:01:43,699
Just sign right here.

899
01:01:44,743 --> 01:01:46,410
[CHUCKLES]

900
01:01:47,037 --> 01:01:51,916
<i>And tomorrow,
life could begin afresh, afresh, afresh.</i>

901
01:01:52,083 --> 01:01:53,334
This way.

902
01:01:57,547 --> 01:01:58,964
Come on. Come.

903
01:02:01,968 --> 01:02:04,595
<i>COMPUTERIZED VOICE:
Welcome to Habitat Mainframe.</i>

904
01:02:05,222 --> 01:02:07,473
<i>Security: maximum.</i>

905
01:02:07,974 --> 01:02:12,019
<i>Accessing Design Database.
Designating skin.</i>

906
01:02:12,187 --> 01:02:13,229
[COMPUTERIZED VOICE
SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

907
01:02:13,396 --> 01:02:15,064
<i>Designating texture.</i>

908
01:02:27,077 --> 01:02:28,994
CHANG:
That's Old Seoul.

909
01:02:30,205 --> 01:02:32,164
If the tides keep rising
at their present rate...

910
01:02:32,332 --> 01:02:35,709
...Neo Seoul will also be underwater
in a hundred years.

911
01:02:40,173 --> 01:02:42,132
[PEOPLE CHANTING]

912
01:03:06,199 --> 01:03:08,158
[CHIMES]

913
01:03:09,452 --> 01:03:11,704
Your food is in here.

914
01:03:12,414 --> 01:03:15,457
It's not what you are used to,
but I think you will like it.

915
01:03:17,294 --> 01:03:18,877
This is your bed.

916
01:03:20,171 --> 01:03:21,839
These are your clothes.

917
01:03:25,927 --> 01:03:30,055
Mine? But these are Pureblood clothes.

918
01:03:30,432 --> 01:03:32,808
No. They are yours.

919
01:03:33,893 --> 01:03:35,728
<i>ARCHIVIST:
Did you know he was Union?</i>

920
01:03:36,313 --> 01:03:38,439
No, but it wouldn't have mattered.

921
01:03:39,190 --> 01:03:40,316
Why not?

922
01:03:41,109 --> 01:03:46,822
Because it was the first time
a Pureblood had shown me kindness.

923
01:03:46,990 --> 01:03:48,615
[PEOPLE CHANTING]

924
01:03:50,785 --> 01:03:51,785
Mama?

925
01:03:52,912 --> 01:03:53,996
Huh?

926
01:03:54,748 --> 01:03:56,332
I hungry.

927
01:03:56,833 --> 01:03:58,834
[CHUCKLING]

928
01:04:04,507 --> 01:04:05,591
ROSE:
Oh, baby.

929
01:04:21,483 --> 01:04:22,941
Do you like them?

930
01:04:25,612 --> 01:04:27,154
You look lovely.

931
01:04:28,114 --> 01:04:29,114
Here.

932
01:04:31,451 --> 01:04:32,701
Come here.

933
01:04:34,120 --> 01:04:37,998
You know, this used to play viddys
but the chip is corrupted.

934
01:04:38,166 --> 01:04:41,794
Stuck in a loop, so I diggied it
and found the rest of it.

935
01:04:41,961 --> 01:04:43,379
The rest of it?

936
01:04:44,005 --> 01:04:46,006
[UPBEAT ORCHESTRAL MUSIC
PLAYING OVER SPEAKERS]

937
01:04:53,014 --> 01:04:55,015
[KEYS JINGLING]

938
01:05:01,815 --> 01:05:04,191
What the ruddy hell
are you doing in my room?

939
01:05:04,359 --> 01:05:08,987
NOAKES: Keys go walkies! Let's give these
to Ms. Judd for safekeeping, shall we?

940
01:05:09,155 --> 01:05:12,449
Leave my things alone,
you pilfering cow!

941
01:05:13,326 --> 01:05:17,204
Because you're new, I shall not make you
eat soap powder, this time.

942
01:05:18,540 --> 01:05:22,292
Be warned, I do not stand for
offensive language in Aurora House.

943
01:05:22,460 --> 01:05:27,381
Not from anyone. And I never make
idle threats, Mr. Cavendish. Never.

944
01:05:27,674 --> 01:05:30,801
I'll talk to you how
I ruddy well like, you thief!

945
01:05:30,969 --> 01:05:34,888
Make me eat soap powder?
I'd like to see you try!

946
01:05:35,056 --> 01:05:37,724
Ooh! Gawd! Bloody hell!

947
01:05:37,892 --> 01:05:40,978
Tsk, tsk, tsk. A disappointing start.

948
01:05:41,688 --> 01:05:44,857
Is this some sort of kinky S & M hotel?

949
01:05:45,525 --> 01:05:50,404
I am Nurse Noakes.
You do not wish to cross me.

950
01:05:55,118 --> 01:05:56,910
[CHATTERING]

951
01:05:57,078 --> 01:06:00,789
HOOKS: I am so sorry to keep you waiting.
Uh, I know it's a Ms...?

952
01:06:00,957 --> 01:06:01,957
REY:
Rey.

953
01:06:02,292 --> 01:06:04,293
<i>Luisa Rey. Spyglass Magazine.</i>

954
01:06:04,461 --> 01:06:05,752
Right. Right.

955
01:06:06,004 --> 01:06:09,548
Luisa, this is Joe Napier,
he's our security chief.

956
01:06:09,716 --> 01:06:11,884
Um, I was surprised to get your query.

957
01:06:12,051 --> 01:06:14,761
I mean, don't get me wrong,
it's a feisty magazine, but, uh...

958
01:06:15,263 --> 01:06:18,098
- Anything you need, sir, just let me know.
HOOKS: Will do.

959
01:06:18,266 --> 01:06:20,684
- Guys, I'm good. Thank you very much.
- Thank you. See you.

960
01:06:20,852 --> 01:06:23,020
Our editor's trying to
spice things up a little bit.

961
01:06:23,229 --> 01:06:25,272
He says the public wants
more substance, so...

962
01:06:25,440 --> 01:06:26,940
Oh. Probably just a fad.

963
01:06:27,108 --> 01:06:28,317
[HOOKS LAUGHS]

964
01:06:28,485 --> 01:06:31,278
Come on, follow me.
Let's start you with the Chicken Ranch.

965
01:06:31,821 --> 01:06:33,780
That is where we keep the Eggheads.

966
01:06:35,366 --> 01:06:38,744
Say hello to Nemo? Okay. Come on.

967
01:06:43,791 --> 01:06:45,292
JUDD:
Hello, Mr. Cavendish.

968
01:06:45,460 --> 01:06:47,085
Feeling super this morning?

969
01:06:47,253 --> 01:06:48,462
No.

970
01:06:48,755 --> 01:06:51,798
I checked in last night believing
that Aurora House was a hotel.

971
01:06:51,966 --> 01:06:55,844
My brother made the booking, you see.
His idea of a practical joke.

972
01:06:56,012 --> 01:07:00,265
But listen, you have a bigger problem
closer to home.

973
01:07:00,934 --> 01:07:03,644
There's some demented bitch
calling herself Noakes...

974
01:07:03,811 --> 01:07:07,314
...rampaging about the place,
impersonating a chambermaid.

975
01:07:08,483 --> 01:07:09,775
But the point is this:

976
01:07:09,943 --> 01:07:13,070
She struck me and she stole my keys.

977
01:07:15,949 --> 01:07:19,117
Right?
I'll need those keys back, straight away.

978
01:07:20,537 --> 01:07:23,163
Aurora House is your home now,
Mr. Cavendish.

979
01:07:23,331 --> 01:07:25,624
Your signature authorizes us to apply-

980
01:07:25,792 --> 01:07:26,792
Signature?

981
01:07:27,001 --> 01:07:30,295
The custody document
you signed last night.

982
01:07:30,463 --> 01:07:32,381
Your residency papers.

983
01:07:32,549 --> 01:07:36,093
No, no, no, no!
That was the hotel register!

984
01:07:36,386 --> 01:07:38,804
Never mind, it's all academic. Oh.

985
01:07:38,972 --> 01:07:42,015
This is gonna make a heck
of a dinner-party story.

986
01:07:42,183 --> 01:07:45,269
Most of our guests get cold feet
on their first mornings.

987
01:07:45,436 --> 01:07:47,062
My keys, please.

988
01:07:47,313 --> 01:07:48,522
Residents are not-

989
01:07:48,690 --> 01:07:51,066
I'm not a ruddy resident!

990
01:07:51,234 --> 01:07:53,944
You'll find temper tantrums
won't help you at Aurora House!

991
01:07:54,112 --> 01:07:56,697
You're breaking the ruddy...

992
01:07:58,324 --> 01:08:02,035
...uh, Anti-Incarceration Act
or some ruddy thing...

993
01:08:02,328 --> 01:08:05,747
...and I will not be subjected
to criminal abuse!

994
01:08:08,084 --> 01:08:12,921
I will not be subjected to criminal abuse.

995
01:08:18,678 --> 01:08:21,346
<i>CAVENDISH LOOK-ALIKE [ON MONITOR]:
Outside, fat snowflakes...</i>

996
01:08:21,514 --> 01:08:23,390
<i>...are falling on slate roofs.</i>

997
01:08:23,891 --> 01:08:26,560
<i>Like Solzhenitsyn laboring in Vermont...</i>

998
01:08:26,728 --> 01:08:29,605
<i>...I shall beaver away in exile.</i>

999
01:08:30,189 --> 01:08:34,026
<i>Unlike Solzhenitsyn, I shan't be alone.</i>

1000
01:08:43,578 --> 01:08:45,078
Off somewhere?

1001
01:08:45,413 --> 01:08:48,915
You bet I am! To the land of the living.

1002
01:08:50,585 --> 01:08:53,170
Soylent Green is people!

1003
01:08:53,588 --> 01:08:57,257
Soylent Green is made of people!

1004
01:08:58,551 --> 01:09:00,927
- Ha-ha-ha.
- Oi! Get back here, you!

1005
01:09:03,056 --> 01:09:04,056
Oi!

1006
01:09:04,223 --> 01:09:06,558
You keep away from me
or I'll be forced to name you...

1007
01:09:06,726 --> 01:09:08,560
...in the police report as an accomplice!

1008
01:09:08,728 --> 01:09:10,687
I have better things to do than this!

1009
01:09:10,897 --> 01:09:15,275
Then go ahead and do them,
you bloody sodding soap-dodger!

1010
01:09:16,903 --> 01:09:17,944
Right!

1011
01:09:19,489 --> 01:09:21,657
[GRUNTING]

1012
01:09:21,824 --> 01:09:25,952
Let go of me, you ruddy
cruddy rugger bugger yob!

1013
01:09:26,120 --> 01:09:27,537
Put me down!

1014
01:09:27,705 --> 01:09:32,167
<i>SONMI-451: "You can maintain power over
people as long as you give them something.</i>

1015
01:09:32,919 --> 01:09:37,798
Rob a man of everything and that man
will no longer be in your power. "

1016
01:09:38,925 --> 01:09:41,718
ARCHIVIST: Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn,
20th century philosopher...

1017
01:09:41,928 --> 01:09:44,638
...Complete Works
banned by Unanimity.

1018
01:09:45,515 --> 01:09:47,432
How do you know about him?

1019
01:09:48,309 --> 01:09:49,518
Hae-Joo.

1020
01:09:50,978 --> 01:09:52,145
SONMl-451:
But...

1021
01:09:52,313 --> 01:09:53,313
...Seer Chang-

1022
01:09:53,481 --> 01:09:55,941
Please, you must call me Hae-Joo.

1023
01:09:57,068 --> 01:09:58,318
Hae-Joo...

1024
01:10:00,363 --> 01:10:02,948
...Fabricants can be excised for this.

1025
01:10:04,492 --> 01:10:07,244
Our survival often demands our courage.

1026
01:10:24,804 --> 01:10:26,596
<i>SONMl-451:
Knowledge is a mirror...</i>

1027
01:10:26,764 --> 01:10:31,727
<i>...and for the first time in my life,
I was allowed to see who I was...</i>

1028
01:10:31,894 --> 01:10:33,895
<i>...and who I might become.</i>

1029
01:10:35,148 --> 01:10:37,149
[MOLYNEUX LAUGHING]

1030
01:10:41,028 --> 01:10:42,154
MOLYNEUX:
Go clean the head.

1031
01:10:44,157 --> 01:10:45,198
Good morning, captain.

1032
01:10:45,366 --> 01:10:49,244
You would help it remain so,
Quillcock, by buggering off.

1033
01:10:49,579 --> 01:10:51,371
I'm afraid I can't do that, sir.

1034
01:10:52,540 --> 01:10:55,459
If I was, I would be unable
to inform you of the stowaway...

1035
01:10:55,626 --> 01:10:57,586
- ... that I have discovered in my cabin.
- Stowaway?!

1036
01:10:57,754 --> 01:11:00,005
I assure you that this Moriori
had no choice.

1037
01:11:00,673 --> 01:11:04,092
Now, he has sworn to me that he is
a first class, able-bodied seaman...

1038
01:11:04,260 --> 01:11:06,720
...capable of earning passage,
if only given a chance.

1039
01:11:06,888 --> 01:11:11,516
A stowaway is a stowaway
even if he shits silver nuggets.

1040
01:11:13,227 --> 01:11:16,188
Did he ever explain anything to you?

1041
01:11:16,814 --> 01:11:21,735
He told me their goal was the creation
of a free-willed Fabricant.

1042
01:11:24,363 --> 01:11:26,239
<i>Yoona had failed.</i>

1043
01:11:27,033 --> 01:11:29,242
<i>I was their last hope.</i>

1044
01:11:33,748 --> 01:11:37,125
I have to say, if all lady journalists
looked like you...

1045
01:11:37,293 --> 01:11:41,421
...I might start to take this women's lib
thing more seriously.

1046
01:11:41,714 --> 01:11:44,299
Well, I'm sure they can
use your support.

1047
01:11:44,467 --> 01:11:45,509
[RECORDER CLICKS]

1048
01:11:45,676 --> 01:11:47,093
Nice.

1049
01:11:47,261 --> 01:11:50,514
Okay, you wait here and I will
go and find someone smarter...

1050
01:11:50,681 --> 01:11:53,183
...who can walk you through
the details of some of your questions.

1051
01:11:53,684 --> 01:11:54,684
Great.

1052
01:11:56,687 --> 01:11:58,688
[PHONE RINGING]

1053
01:12:11,035 --> 01:12:12,160
[RINGING CONTINUES]

1054
01:12:13,287 --> 01:12:16,540
[WHISPERS]
Okay, what would Dad do?

1055
01:12:22,255 --> 01:12:24,256
[PHONE RINGING]

1056
01:12:25,383 --> 01:12:28,426
DENHOLME: Who the bloody hell is this?
Have you any idea what time it is?

1057
01:12:28,594 --> 01:12:30,220
Denny? It's me, Tim.

1058
01:12:30,388 --> 01:12:31,596
<i>Timothy?</i>

1059
01:12:32,390 --> 01:12:33,890
Where are you?

1060
01:12:34,100 --> 01:12:36,685
<i>I think you ruddy well
know where I am!</i>

1061
01:12:36,853 --> 01:12:39,145
But residents aren't allowed
access to phones.

1062
01:12:39,313 --> 01:12:41,189
<i>Has someone smuggled one in?</i>

1063
01:12:41,357 --> 01:12:42,649
You know the rules?

1064
01:12:42,817 --> 01:12:44,609
<i>I helped write them, Timmy.</i>

1065
01:12:44,777 --> 01:12:47,904
<i>I have been a principal investor
in Aurora House for 12 years.</i>

1066
01:12:48,197 --> 01:12:52,033
It's incredibly lucrative. You can't believe
what people will pay to lock up their parents.

1067
01:12:54,662 --> 01:12:56,538
Look, Den. You've had your fun.

1068
01:12:56,706 --> 01:12:59,916
I think it's high time you put an end
to this little game of yours.

1069
01:13:00,084 --> 01:13:04,337
Ha, ha. No, no, Timmy.
My fun has just begun.

1070
01:13:04,505 --> 01:13:05,964
<i>What are you talking about?</i>

1071
01:13:06,132 --> 01:13:08,633
I'm your brother.
Why are you doing this to me?

1072
01:13:08,801 --> 01:13:11,428
<i>I think a better question
in this instance would be:</i>

1073
01:13:11,637 --> 01:13:13,513
<i>What have you done to deserve this?</i>

1074
01:13:13,681 --> 01:13:15,140
I don't know what you mean.

1075
01:13:15,308 --> 01:13:17,684
Oh, come now, dear brother,
don't insult me.

1076
01:13:17,852 --> 01:13:21,563
You can't think that I didn't know
about you and Georgette!

1077
01:13:22,899 --> 01:13:24,232
Georgette?

1078
01:13:26,861 --> 01:13:31,239
Look, Den, I didn't mean to hurt you.

1079
01:13:31,407 --> 01:13:36,077
I'm afraid your penance has come due,
Timbo. It's time to account for your crimes.

1080
01:13:36,871 --> 01:13:37,871
Denholme...

1081
01:13:38,581 --> 01:13:41,166
...I'm- I'm so, so sorry.

1082
01:13:41,334 --> 01:13:43,501
<i>No, no, no.
There's no need to apologize.</i>

1083
01:13:43,669 --> 01:13:46,087
<i>Your exile is more
than enough reparation.</i>

1084
01:13:46,255 --> 01:13:50,675
Although, ha, ha, I do have my fingers
crossed for a scenario involving you...

1085
01:13:50,843 --> 01:13:52,761
...Nurse Noakes and a broom handle.

1086
01:13:52,929 --> 01:13:55,305
Cheerio, Timmy. Bye-bye now.

1087
01:13:56,933 --> 01:13:58,475
Sends his love.

1088
01:14:08,986 --> 01:14:11,863
I would like to ask about
the night of your arrest.

1089
01:14:13,407 --> 01:14:16,910
I remember listening to his heart beat.

1090
01:14:18,496 --> 01:14:22,207
<i>Your heart beats much slower than ours.</i>

1091
01:14:22,917 --> 01:14:25,877
<i>There is a gentleness to the sound.</i>

1092
01:14:26,379 --> 01:14:29,547
<i>I find it comforting.</i>

1093
01:14:29,715 --> 01:14:30,715
[HEART BEATING]

1094
01:14:30,883 --> 01:14:31,883
[KNOCKING ON DOOR]

1095
01:14:32,051 --> 01:14:33,301
Vyvyan, what time is it?

1096
01:14:33,469 --> 01:14:35,136
I don't know. Who cares?

1097
01:14:35,429 --> 01:14:38,682
I've heard a melody, boy. For violin.

1098
01:14:38,849 --> 01:14:39,975
Quick.

1099
01:14:40,309 --> 01:14:41,893
Find a pen!

1100
01:14:43,229 --> 01:14:44,980
I heard it in a dream.

1101
01:14:45,147 --> 01:14:47,565
I was in a nightmarish café...

1102
01:14:47,733 --> 01:14:49,609
...blaring, bright light...

1103
01:14:49,777 --> 01:14:52,404
...but underground, and no way out.

1104
01:14:53,990 --> 01:14:55,573
And the waitresses...

1105
01:14:56,117 --> 01:14:57,784
...they all had the same face.

1106
01:14:58,536 --> 01:15:03,456
There was music playing, but unlike
any music I've ever heard in my life.

1107
01:15:07,128 --> 01:15:08,628
It began...

1108
01:15:09,755 --> 01:15:10,755
It began...

1109
01:15:11,966 --> 01:15:13,091
Wait.

1110
01:15:15,052 --> 01:15:18,888
It was so clear a minute ago.

1111
01:15:19,807 --> 01:15:23,268
Help me, Robert! Help me!
It's slipping away.

1112
01:15:25,688 --> 01:15:27,063
I've lost it.

1113
01:15:28,524 --> 01:15:30,483
It'll come to you, sir.

1114
01:15:30,818 --> 01:15:33,278
The minute you stop trying to find it,
it'll find you.

1115
01:15:33,446 --> 01:15:35,488
You are naïve, Robert.

1116
01:15:38,409 --> 01:15:39,993
I am anything but.

1117
01:15:40,453 --> 01:15:43,413
There is a gulf between these chairs.

1118
01:15:44,123 --> 01:15:46,291
What you want is no different
from what I want.

1119
01:15:47,668 --> 01:15:49,627
The gulf is an illusion.

1120
01:15:50,379 --> 01:15:51,379
[CHUCKLES]

1121
01:15:51,547 --> 01:15:53,631
<i>FROBISHER:
How do I describe that night, Sixsmith?</i>

1122
01:15:54,300 --> 01:15:58,303
<i>What had happened between Vyvyan and I
transcended language.</i>

1123
01:15:59,847 --> 01:16:03,767
<i>It was music that poured from his eyes,
that breathed from his lips.</i>

1124
01:16:03,934 --> 01:16:05,935
[GASPS]

1125
01:16:06,479 --> 01:16:09,230
<i>Music as beautiful
as any I have ever heard.</i>

1126
01:16:09,732 --> 01:16:11,399
[GASPS]

1127
01:16:15,863 --> 01:16:17,655
What are you doing in here?

1128
01:16:19,784 --> 01:16:21,493
[INDISTINCT CHATTER OVER DEVICE]

1129
01:16:21,952 --> 01:16:23,161
They found us.

1130
01:16:28,000 --> 01:16:29,167
[ALARM BUZZING]

1131
01:16:50,856 --> 01:16:54,275
I'll be with you the whole way.
Now focus on me.

1132
01:16:58,989 --> 01:17:00,198
SONMl-451:
Hae-Joo!

1133
01:17:00,533 --> 01:17:02,367
Stay with me.

1134
01:17:02,868 --> 01:17:05,036
Right here. I won't let you go.

1135
01:17:14,171 --> 01:17:15,755
[SHRIEKS]

1136
01:17:18,467 --> 01:17:20,218
[MAN SHOUTING INDISTINCTLY]

1137
01:17:24,056 --> 01:17:25,431
- Mr. Ewing!
EWING: It's all right.

1138
01:17:25,599 --> 01:17:27,892
I've talked to the captain.
He'll hear your case.

1139
01:17:28,644 --> 01:17:30,228
What's your name, boy?

1140
01:17:31,063 --> 01:17:32,564
Autua, sir.

1141
01:17:33,440 --> 01:17:36,901
This Christian gentleman,
who knows nothing about ships...

1142
01:17:37,069 --> 01:17:39,154
...tells me that you're a first-class sailor.

1143
01:17:39,321 --> 01:17:40,989
[MEN CHUCKLE]

1144
01:17:41,407 --> 01:17:42,490
Very well.

1145
01:17:42,658 --> 01:17:44,951
Let's see you lower the main topsail.

1146
01:17:50,666 --> 01:17:53,126
Mr. Roderick, my bottle is empty.

1147
01:18:03,262 --> 01:18:06,097
Mr. Boerhaave, ready my piece.

1148
01:18:06,265 --> 01:18:10,685
What? Sir, you gave me
your word, captain.

1149
01:18:11,103 --> 01:18:13,354
Please. You can't do this!

1150
01:18:13,522 --> 01:18:16,941
Nobody tells me what I can
and cannot do on my own ship...

1151
01:18:17,109 --> 01:18:19,444
...especially when it concerns
nigger stowaways.

1152
01:18:21,655 --> 01:18:22,655
[MEN SHOUTING]

1153
01:18:22,823 --> 01:18:24,282
MAN 1:
Get them!

1154
01:18:26,702 --> 01:18:28,119
[SONMl-451 SHRIEKS]

1155
01:18:31,373 --> 01:18:32,415
[MAN 2 GROANS]

1156
01:18:34,460 --> 01:18:36,127
[MEN GROAN]

1157
01:18:45,804 --> 01:18:46,846
OLD SALTY DOG:
Cap'n, look!

1158
01:18:47,014 --> 01:18:49,015
He's got fish-hooks for toes.

1159
01:18:49,183 --> 01:18:52,310
Mr. Boerhaave,
do not make a mess of my deck.

1160
01:19:07,409 --> 01:19:08,868
SONMl-451:
No!

1161
01:19:16,168 --> 01:19:17,168
Hae-Joo!

1162
01:19:27,680 --> 01:19:29,138
Captain, please.

1163
01:19:32,559 --> 01:19:35,228
Look, if you could
just hear me out, please.

1164
01:19:42,528 --> 01:19:43,945
MAN:
Look!

1165
01:19:51,745 --> 01:19:55,373
Ha-ha-ha.
The darkie's salt as I am!

1166
01:19:58,210 --> 01:19:59,669
Mr. Boerhaave?

1167
01:20:01,046 --> 01:20:04,882
It appears we have an addition
to our crew. Be sure he earns his keep.

1168
01:20:07,678 --> 01:20:08,886
SACHS:
Mr. Hooks!

1169
01:20:10,180 --> 01:20:11,180
Found her.

1170
01:20:11,473 --> 01:20:14,892
She was in the chem labs
looking for the bathroom.

1171
01:20:15,686 --> 01:20:16,728
Ah.

1172
01:20:16,895 --> 01:20:20,315
Well, perfect. Then why don't you
take over from here, Sachs?

1173
01:20:20,733 --> 01:20:23,151
You know, introduce our little tribe...

1174
01:20:23,319 --> 01:20:27,071
...and guide Ms. Rey through the, uh-
Through the tower.

1175
01:20:27,364 --> 01:20:29,907
SACHS: Funny thing is,
I'm not even supposed to be here.

1176
01:20:30,242 --> 01:20:31,784
I was meant to be in Seoul...

1177
01:20:31,952 --> 01:20:34,829
...but the air-traffic-controller strike
screwed everything up...

1178
01:20:34,997 --> 01:20:37,248
...so now I'm on
the pond-jumper tonight.

1179
01:20:37,416 --> 01:20:40,251
You ever think the universe
was against you?

1180
01:20:40,753 --> 01:20:42,253
- All the time.
- Mm.

1181
01:20:43,297 --> 01:20:44,297
You mind?

1182
01:20:45,257 --> 01:20:46,507
I'm cool.

1183
01:20:47,134 --> 01:20:49,135
[REY CHUCKLES]

1184
01:20:55,768 --> 01:20:57,769
[SACHS CHUCKLES]

1185
01:20:59,938 --> 01:21:01,939
You seem nervous, Isaac.

1186
01:21:02,107 --> 01:21:04,150
- Do I make you nervous?
- No.

1187
01:21:05,944 --> 01:21:07,945
Actually, just the opposite.

1188
01:21:15,788 --> 01:21:17,789
[COUGHING]

1189
01:21:19,708 --> 01:21:21,167
[CHUCKLES]

1190
01:21:26,465 --> 01:21:28,758
Are you gonna tell me why
you covered for me?

1191
01:21:32,054 --> 01:21:34,055
[BANGING]

1192
01:21:34,973 --> 01:21:39,644
<i>CAVENDISH: "Freedom, "
the fatuous jingle of our civilization.</i>

1193
01:21:40,896 --> 01:21:45,316
<i>But only those deprived of it have
the barest inkling of what it really is.</i>

1194
01:21:50,614 --> 01:21:51,739
[DOOR OPENS]

1195
01:22:03,377 --> 01:22:07,338
There's much disagreement
on what should be done with you.

1196
01:22:07,798 --> 01:22:11,592
The Corprocrats want you
euthanized as a deviant.

1197
01:22:11,760 --> 01:22:14,095
The Manufacturer is demanding
a period of study.

1198
01:22:14,263 --> 01:22:19,183
The psychogenomicists are screaming
for an immediate cerebral vivisection.

1199
01:22:19,977 --> 01:22:25,189
However, the problem you create
is a political one...

1200
01:22:27,943 --> 01:22:30,528
...which means you're my problem.

1201
01:22:32,364 --> 01:22:35,199
I find it intriguing to imagine that...

1202
01:22:35,409 --> 01:22:39,704
...beneath these perfectly
engineered features...

1203
01:22:39,872 --> 01:22:43,708
...are thoughts that terrify
the whole of Unanimity.

1204
01:22:47,838 --> 01:22:51,799
I'm not afraid of such thoughts
because I do not fear the truth.

1205
01:22:53,510 --> 01:22:57,054
There's a natural order
to this world, Fabricant...

1206
01:22:58,015 --> 01:23:01,726
...and the truth is,
this order must be protected.

1207
01:23:14,531 --> 01:23:18,201
Inform the Archivist
and prepare her for Excisement.

1208
01:23:18,368 --> 01:23:20,203
MAN: Yes, sir.
SONMl-451: Sir?

1209
01:23:23,540 --> 01:23:26,209
Can you tell me what happened
to Hae-Joo Chang?

1210
01:23:26,960 --> 01:23:29,170
Killed, I was told.

1211
01:23:33,342 --> 01:23:35,343
[DOOR CLOSES]

1212
01:23:51,735 --> 01:23:55,780
GOOSE: All these recent excitements,
really, Adam, it's far too much.

1213
01:23:55,948 --> 01:23:57,949
You need to rest.

1214
01:24:11,964 --> 01:24:15,216
I had a girlfriend once.

1215
01:24:15,551 --> 01:24:19,387
She kept trying to get me
to read Carlos Castaneda.

1216
01:24:20,097 --> 01:24:21,430
You ever read any of that shit?

1217
01:24:21,598 --> 01:24:22,932
Oh, yeah.

1218
01:24:25,269 --> 01:24:27,770
But the relationship was doomed.

1219
01:24:28,021 --> 01:24:31,816
Every time she brought up any
of that karma, past life stuff...

1220
01:24:32,025 --> 01:24:34,318
...I couldn't stop myself from laughing.

1221
01:24:35,946 --> 01:24:37,280
And yet...

1222
01:24:38,657 --> 01:24:40,449
...I can't explain it...

1223
01:24:41,493 --> 01:24:44,412
...but I knew when I opened that door...

1224
01:24:57,551 --> 01:25:00,595
They destroyed most of the copies
of the report.

1225
01:25:02,931 --> 01:25:04,265
Most?

1226
01:25:07,769 --> 01:25:10,104
There's no good choice here, is there?

1227
01:25:10,272 --> 01:25:12,940
If I help you, I could lose my job...

1228
01:25:13,817 --> 01:25:15,192
...or worse.

1229
01:25:17,070 --> 01:25:20,740
If I don't, a lot of people...

1230
01:25:22,993 --> 01:25:25,202
Yeah, it'll be worse than worse.

1231
01:25:27,539 --> 01:25:32,835
You have to do whatever you can't not do.

1232
01:25:35,255 --> 01:25:37,298
[OLD GEORGIE SLOWLY EXHALES
THEN LAUGHING]

1233
01:25:39,134 --> 01:25:41,469
Something praying on you, Zachry?

1234
01:25:42,638 --> 01:25:45,348
You really ain't feary 'bout meetin'
Old Georgie on the summit?

1235
01:25:45,515 --> 01:25:48,726
More scaresome 'bout the weather
than any devil.

1236
01:25:52,397 --> 01:25:54,065
You cog he's real?

1237
01:25:54,816 --> 01:25:57,652
Who tripped the Fall,
if not Old Georgie?

1238
01:25:59,655 --> 01:26:01,322
True-true?

1239
01:26:05,202 --> 01:26:06,619
The Old Uns.

1240
01:26:07,746 --> 01:26:09,622
That's jus' a rope o' smoke.

1241
01:26:09,873 --> 01:26:12,166
Old Uns got the Smart.

1242
01:26:13,251 --> 01:26:15,419
They mastered sick and seeds...

1243
01:26:15,921 --> 01:26:18,089
...mak'd mir'cles 'n fly 'cross the sky.

1244
01:26:18,256 --> 01:26:21,425
True. All true.
But they got somethin' else.

1245
01:26:21,885 --> 01:26:26,263
A hunger'n their hearts,
hunger that's stronger'n all their Smart.

1246
01:26:27,766 --> 01:26:29,684
Hunger? For what?

1247
01:26:31,520 --> 01:26:33,270
A hunger for more.

1248
01:26:34,898 --> 01:26:39,944
<i>SACHS: Belief, like fear or love,
is a force to be understood...</i>

1249
01:26:40,112 --> 01:26:42,571
<i>...as we understand
the Theory of Relativity...</i>

1250
01:26:42,739 --> 01:26:44,448
<i>...and Principles of Uncertainty...</i>

1251
01:26:46,493 --> 01:26:50,204
<i>...phenomena that determine
the course of our lives.</i>

1252
01:26:51,832 --> 01:26:55,626
<i>Yesterday, my life was headed
in one direction.</i>

1253
01:26:59,297 --> 01:27:02,508
<i>Today, it is headed in another.</i>

1254
01:27:03,385 --> 01:27:07,638
<i>Yesterday, I believed I would never
have done what I did today.</i>

1255
01:27:09,349 --> 01:27:12,768
<i>These forces that often remake
time and space...</i>

1256
01:27:13,353 --> 01:27:17,022
<i>...that can shape and alter
who we imagine ourself to be...</i>

1257
01:27:17,190 --> 01:27:21,902
<i>...begin long before we are born
and continue after we perish.</i>

1258
01:27:26,950 --> 01:27:29,535
<i>Our lives and our choices...</i>

1259
01:27:30,120 --> 01:27:32,079
<i>...like quantum trajectories...</i>

1260
01:27:32,247 --> 01:27:35,082
<i>...are understood moment to moment.</i>

1261
01:27:36,001 --> 01:27:37,585
<i>At each point of intersection...</i>

1262
01:27:38,170 --> 01:27:40,129
<i>...each encounter...</i>

1263
01:27:41,047 --> 01:27:44,717
<i>...suggests a new potential direction.</i>

1264
01:27:47,596 --> 01:27:52,516
<i>Proposition: I have fallen
in love with Luisa Rey.</i>

1265
01:27:54,227 --> 01:27:55,853
<i>Is this possible?</i>

1266
01:27:56,688 --> 01:27:59,482
<i>I just met her, and yet...</i>

1267
01:28:00,233 --> 01:28:06,030
<i>...I feel like something important
has happened to me.</i>

1268
01:28:16,583 --> 01:28:17,917
AYRS:
That's it.

1269
01:28:18,794 --> 01:28:21,003
The music from my dream.

1270
01:28:24,007 --> 01:28:26,008
[STREETLIGHT CRACKLING]

1271
01:28:49,074 --> 01:28:51,075
[GRUNTS]

1272
01:29:02,629 --> 01:29:05,756
AYRS: This is from my dream.
That night I came to your room...

1273
01:29:05,924 --> 01:29:06,924
[PLAYING PIANO]

1274
01:29:07,092 --> 01:29:09,635
...this is the music I heard in my head.

1275
01:29:10,136 --> 01:29:11,804
Somehow I gave it to you.

1276
01:29:11,972 --> 01:29:14,640
I've been working on this piece
for weeks now...

1277
01:29:14,808 --> 01:29:18,978
...and I suspect you heard it
and incorporated it into your dream.

1278
01:29:20,188 --> 01:29:22,815
I call it the "Cloud Atlas Sextet. "

1279
01:29:26,736 --> 01:29:30,406
This is obviously the result
of our collaboration.

1280
01:29:31,575 --> 01:29:33,492
The "Atlas," I believe...

1281
01:29:34,494 --> 01:29:37,329
...is the only thing I have done
in my life that has value.

1282
01:29:39,416 --> 01:29:41,667
Yet I know I could not have written it...

1283
01:29:43,128 --> 01:29:44,962
...if I hadn't met you.

1284
01:29:45,630 --> 01:29:49,383
There are whole movements in the "Atlas"
that I wrote imagining us...

1285
01:29:49,551 --> 01:29:53,846
...meeting again and again
in different lives, in different ages.

1286
01:29:54,014 --> 01:29:55,180
Yes.

1287
01:29:56,266 --> 01:30:00,686
Something as important as this
cannot be described as yours...

1288
01:30:01,187 --> 01:30:02,563
...or mine.

1289
01:30:03,440 --> 01:30:05,190
It is ours.

1290
01:30:12,240 --> 01:30:14,408
That is exactly how I feel, Vyvyan.

1291
01:30:32,010 --> 01:30:33,886
[LAUGHING]

1292
01:30:35,430 --> 01:30:36,847
I'm sorry, I-

1293
01:30:37,474 --> 01:30:38,557
I thought-

1294
01:30:38,725 --> 01:30:41,101
You thought? You thought what?

1295
01:30:41,353 --> 01:30:46,607
That I might fancy a little buggering
from a fine young dandy like yourself?

1296
01:30:46,775 --> 01:30:48,776
[AYRS LAUGHING]

1297
01:30:50,654 --> 01:30:52,321
I'll pack my bags
and be gone by morning.

1298
01:30:52,489 --> 01:30:55,366
You'll do no such thing.
You'll leave only when I say you can leave.

1299
01:30:55,533 --> 01:30:59,870
You will continue working
on Vyvyan Ayrs' "Cloud Atlas. "

1300
01:31:00,038 --> 01:31:03,123
When it is finished,
then I will decide what to do with you.

1301
01:31:03,291 --> 01:31:05,084
You can't keep me here! I'm leaving.

1302
01:31:05,543 --> 01:31:07,086
Good luck with your composing.

1303
01:31:07,253 --> 01:31:09,129
I'm sure a sterile old fuck like yourself...

1304
01:31:09,297 --> 01:31:12,049
...is still capable of something
completely inmemorable.

1305
01:31:12,217 --> 01:31:14,760
I suggest you think about this, Robert.

1306
01:31:14,928 --> 01:31:17,054
Think about reputation.

1307
01:31:18,098 --> 01:31:20,432
Reputation is everything in our society.

1308
01:31:20,600 --> 01:31:25,187
Yours, my disinherited reprobate,
has expired.

1309
01:31:25,563 --> 01:31:29,692
Did you not think that we would inquire
about someone living under our roof?

1310
01:31:29,859 --> 01:31:32,695
Mackerras himself wrote, and I quote:

1311
01:31:32,862 --> 01:31:34,446
"He is a prostitute...

1312
01:31:34,614 --> 01:31:38,867
...whose liaisons with perverts
and sodomites were commonplace...

1313
01:31:39,035 --> 01:31:42,329
...in his brief and forgettable
career at Caius.

1314
01:31:42,497 --> 01:31:46,458
Lock up the silverware. " Unquote.

1315
01:31:49,212 --> 01:31:50,254
Be warned:

1316
01:31:50,588 --> 01:31:55,050
Leave here without my consent
and all the musical society will know...

1317
01:31:55,218 --> 01:31:58,470
...of the degenerate Robert Frobisher.

1318
01:31:59,806 --> 01:32:01,223
After that...

1319
01:32:01,891 --> 01:32:06,061
...even if you compose one of
the greatest symphonies ever written...

1320
01:32:06,813 --> 01:32:09,231
...no one will hear it...

1321
01:32:09,399 --> 01:32:13,527
...because no one will want
anything to do with you.

1322
01:32:27,292 --> 01:32:29,293
[BOTH GRUNTING]

1323
01:32:52,859 --> 01:32:54,526
I won't let you go again.

1324
01:33:00,784 --> 01:33:03,160
I told you he'd come.

1325
01:33:03,787 --> 01:33:05,120
<i>FROBISHER:
Two things became clear.</i>

1326
01:33:05,747 --> 01:33:08,082
<i>Hanging myself from
Edinburgh's flagpole was preferable...</i>

1327
01:33:08,249 --> 01:33:11,293
<i>...to letting that parasite
plunder my talents a day longer.</i>

1328
01:33:11,878 --> 01:33:13,921
<i>I must complete my sextet.</i>

1329
01:33:14,089 --> 01:33:17,382
<i>I can't do it here,
so tonight I plan to make my escape.</i>

1330
01:33:29,896 --> 01:33:32,064
<i>COMPUTERIZED VOICE:
Please stand by for vehicle scan.</i>

1331
01:33:32,232 --> 01:33:34,233
[COMPUTERIZED VOICE
SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

1332
01:33:34,651 --> 01:33:36,443
<i>Clearance granted.</i>

1333
01:33:55,630 --> 01:33:58,173
Any jailbreak's a risky proposition.

1334
01:33:58,633 --> 01:34:03,178
One little cock-up and we're dangling
at Her Majesty's pleasure.

1335
01:34:03,346 --> 01:34:05,430
I know, I know.

1336
01:34:05,640 --> 01:34:07,850
BLACKSMITH:
We could use code names...

1337
01:34:08,017 --> 01:34:11,228
...but truth is, I'd probably be
the first to forget mine.

1338
01:34:12,480 --> 01:34:15,858
So, Mr. Cavendish, Ernie Blacksmith.

1339
01:34:16,526 --> 01:34:21,280
This is Mr. Meeks,
and my girl, Veronica Costello.

1340
01:34:22,490 --> 01:34:23,657
To trust.

1341
01:34:23,908 --> 01:34:25,075
To trust.

1342
01:34:26,369 --> 01:34:28,787
I know, I know.

1343
01:34:30,582 --> 01:34:34,877
What about the parrot, then?
If ever there was a likely songbird.

1344
01:34:35,044 --> 01:34:38,046
Mr. Meeks is a fine
and honorable gentleman.

1345
01:34:38,214 --> 01:34:39,673
He would never betray us.

1346
01:34:39,841 --> 01:34:42,843
Besides, no one's ever heard him
say anything else.

1347
01:34:43,011 --> 01:34:44,803
I know, I know.

1348
01:34:45,597 --> 01:34:47,264
BLACKSMITH:
Question is, old man:

1349
01:34:47,891 --> 01:34:50,058
Think you're up to snuff?

1350
01:34:59,569 --> 01:35:01,570
[MUMBLES INDISTINCTLY]

1351
01:35:05,241 --> 01:35:07,993
<i>MAN [OVER DEVICE]: Unanimity requires
compliance. We have a security code red.</i>

1352
01:35:08,161 --> 01:35:10,120
<i>Prepare to be boarded.</i>

1353
01:35:10,288 --> 01:35:11,830
[WHISPERS]
What are we going to do?

1354
01:35:12,373 --> 01:35:14,666
Stay calm, stay calm.

1355
01:35:15,877 --> 01:35:18,837
<i>FROBISHER:
"It will end in tears. " You warned me.</i>

1356
01:35:19,464 --> 01:35:21,632
<i>I suppose I'm as hopeless
as Adam Ewing...</i>

1357
01:35:21,799 --> 01:35:25,052
<i>...oblivious to all the unspeakable
forms lying in wait...</i>

1358
01:35:25,220 --> 01:35:27,471
<i>...blind to the fact his friend
is poisoning him.</i>

1359
01:35:27,639 --> 01:35:29,473
Henry, please.

1360
01:35:29,641 --> 01:35:32,017
The idea of losing this ring
distresses me beyond measure.

1361
01:35:32,185 --> 01:35:34,353
Don't be a silly puffin, Adam.
I'm sure your wife...

1362
01:35:34,520 --> 01:35:36,813
...would set your health
above a gold loop.

1363
01:35:37,357 --> 01:35:41,276
I have seen the onset for dropsy
and it is not a pretty sight.

1364
01:35:41,945 --> 01:35:43,362
[EWING GRUNTS]

1365
01:35:43,529 --> 01:35:46,990
I know an excellent Spanish goldsmith
who works with such alacrity...

1366
01:35:47,158 --> 01:35:49,826
...that your Tilda may not have
to know this was ever removed.

1367
01:35:51,955 --> 01:35:52,955
CAVENDISH:
Give it to me!

1368
01:35:53,122 --> 01:35:55,332
Get your fuckin' hands off my pudding!

1369
01:35:55,500 --> 01:35:58,252
It's not your pudding,
you Alzheimer's lout!

1370
01:35:58,419 --> 01:36:00,087
You've already eaten your pudding!

1371
01:36:00,505 --> 01:36:01,505
WITHERS:
Stop it!

1372
01:36:01,673 --> 01:36:04,424
CAVENDISH: Give it to me!
BLACKSMITH: Leggo, ya dos cunt!

1373
01:36:04,592 --> 01:36:05,842
CAVENDISH:
Don't you talk to me like that!

1374
01:36:06,010 --> 01:36:08,303
BLACKSMITH: I'll speak to you any way I-
- You better get in here.

1375
01:36:08,471 --> 01:36:11,807
CAVENDISH: It doesn't mean you can have
two puddings just because you've already-

1376
01:36:11,975 --> 01:36:13,684
WITHERS:
Stop it now! Unh!

1377
01:36:14,978 --> 01:36:16,603
CAVENDISH:
You ruddy idiot!

1378
01:36:16,771 --> 01:36:18,647
You think you can get away with that?

1379
01:36:18,815 --> 01:36:21,483
[CAVENDISH & BLACKSMITH
SHOUTING INDISTINCTLY]

1380
01:36:25,071 --> 01:36:26,071
WITHERS:
Stop it!

1381
01:36:28,241 --> 01:36:29,241
I said, stop it!

1382
01:36:29,409 --> 01:36:30,492
Mr. Cavendish!

1383
01:36:30,660 --> 01:36:32,661
[OBJECT CLATTERING]

1384
01:36:40,169 --> 01:36:42,629
<i>FROBISHER:
The room stank of bitter medicine.</i>

1385
01:36:49,637 --> 01:36:52,347
<i>Curiously heavy things, guns.</i>

1386
01:36:52,807 --> 01:36:54,850
<i>Why did I take it exactly?</i>

1387
01:36:55,310 --> 01:36:56,643
<i>Can't say.</i>

1388
01:36:57,478 --> 01:37:00,647
<i>An intuition, a sense of significance...</i>

1389
01:37:02,191 --> 01:37:04,151
<i>...that from this point on...</i>

1390
01:37:05,653 --> 01:37:07,362
<i>...there was no going back.</i>

1391
01:37:22,503 --> 01:37:23,962
Okay.

1392
01:37:25,506 --> 01:37:28,675
Sussin' them clouds, we run out time.

1393
01:37:32,347 --> 01:37:35,349
Hey, don't need no smart rope.

1394
01:37:35,683 --> 01:37:36,975
Yea.

1395
01:37:37,143 --> 01:37:40,187
See you fall, I catch you.

1396
01:37:57,038 --> 01:37:58,705
MAN 1:
Hands in the air!

1397
01:37:59,290 --> 01:38:00,665
- Step off the vehicle.
MAN 2: Get down!

1398
01:38:00,833 --> 01:38:01,875
[CHANG SPEAKING
IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

1399
01:38:02,043 --> 01:38:03,710
MAN 1:
Get down! Now!

1400
01:38:05,463 --> 01:38:06,546
[MAN 3 SHOUTING INDISTINCTLY]

1401
01:38:07,131 --> 01:38:08,507
MAN 1:
Fucking migrant monkey-talk.

